(8568, 276, 'What does a project risk register typically contain?', 'Identified risks, their likelihood, impact and mitigation plans', 'A list of compiler flags', 'The source code of all modules', 'The password of every account', 'A', 'A risk register logs each risk with its probability, impact and a plan to mitigate or accept it.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8569, 276, 'Which of the following is a legitimate reason to revise the project plan mid-course?', 'A discovered dependency on an external service that changes the schedule', 'Because the team is bored', 'To make the document longer', 'To avoid writing code', 'A', 'Plans are living documents that should be updated when new facts, such as external dependencies, emerge.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8570, 276, 'How does a good project plan help during the final evaluation?', 'It provides a baseline to compare actual progress against planned progress', 'It replaces the need for a demo', 'It hides missing features', 'It guarantees a perfect score', 'A', 'A plan acts as the baseline so evaluators can see whether the project achieved what was originally committed.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8571, 276, 'A key dependency that your whole schedule relies on is delivered two weeks late. Which action best recovers the plan?', 'Re-baseline the schedule, crash or fast-track remaining tasks, and inform stakeholders', 'Pretend the date never changed', 'Delete the dependency from records', 'Ask for double credit', 'A', 'Re-baselining with compression techniques and transparent stakeholder communication restores a realistic, defensible plan.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8572, 276, 'Which scheduling approach is most resilient when the effort for research-heavy tasks is highly uncertain?', 'A probabilistic method such as PERT or Monte Carlo that models optimistic, likely and pessimistic durations', 'A single fixed estimate with no buffer', 'Assuming every task takes exactly one day', 'Never planning in advance', 'A', 'Probabilistic techniques explicitly model uncertainty so the plan reflects the real spread of possible durations.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8573, 276, 'A sponsor requests a scope reduction to cut cost. Which aspect should you re-negotiate or review first?', 'The project charter and baseline scope statement', 'The colour of the logo', 'The number of keyboards', 'The meeting room size', 'A', 'Any scope change must be reconciled against the charter and baseline so goals, deliverables and acceptance stay aligned.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8574, 276, 'Which practice best prevents ''scope creep'' in a capstone project?', 'A formal change control board and traceability from requirements to deliverables', 'Allowing anyone to add features freely', 'Never documenting decisions', 'Merging all features at the last minute', 'A', 'Change control with requirement traceability makes unapproved additions visible and prevents silent expansion of scope.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8575, 276, 'A milestone review reveals you have spent 60 percent of the budget but completed 30 percent of the work. What does this indicate?', 'Cost performance is over budget and the earned value is behind plan', 'The project is running ahead of schedule', 'The budget is under-used', 'The scope grew but that is fine', 'A', 'Earned value analysis shows that spending exceeds the value of work completed, signalling a cost and schedule problem.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8576, 276, 'Which buffer placement is most effective in a critical chain plan?', 'A project buffer at the end of the chain plus feeding buffers at converging paths', 'Buffers at random points', 'No buffers at all', 'One huge buffer in the middle', 'A', 'Critical chain methods place a project buffer at the chain end and feeding buffers at merging paths to absorb delay.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8577, 276, 'A stakeholder demands a feature that conflicts with your stated objectives. What is the best professional response?', 'Discuss the conflict against the scope statement and seek an agreed amendment', 'Implement it secretly', 'Ignore the stakeholder', 'Quit the project', 'A', 'Professionally surfacing the conflict and negotiating an amendment protects the project from uncontrolled scope change.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8578, 276, 'Which tool best supports tracking dependencies and resource assignments across a multi-module capstone?', 'A project management tool with a WBS, Gantt view and resource levelling', 'A single sticky note', 'A voice memo', 'A spreadsheet with no structure', 'A', 'Project management software tracks task dependencies and resource allocation centrally, making the plan auditable.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8579, 276, 'Why is a risk-based contingency plan superior to promising zero risk in a project plan?', 'It prepares the team for plausible failures and defines pre-agreed responses', 'It eliminates all risk', 'It removes the need for testing', 'It guarantees approval', 'A', 'Contingency planning acknowledges uncertainty and defines responses, so the team reacts quickly when risks materialise.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8580, 276, 'When compressing a schedule, which trade-off is most common when adding team members to a task?', 'Communication overhead grows and may slow progress beyond a point', 'Cost automatically decreases', 'Quality always improves', 'Scope doubles', 'A', 'Adding people adds coordination cost, so beyond a point it can slow a task rather than speed it up.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8581, 277, 'Which version control tool is most commonly used to track changes to project source code?', 'Git', 'Word', 'Paint', 'Calculator', 'A', 'Git tracks source code changes, enabling branching, merging and a full history of edits.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8582, 277, 'Which development model builds the system in small repeating cycles with user feedback?', 'Agile or iterative development', 'Waterfall without reviews', 'Random coding', 'Copy-paste development', 'A', 'Agile development delivers in short iterations, incorporating feedback continuously.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8583, 277, 'What is a commit in version control?', 'A recorded snapshot of changes to the repository', 'A type of SQL join', 'A network packet', 'A CPU cache line', 'A', 'A commit stores a snapshot of the project files with a message describing the changes made.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8584, 277, 'Which file lists the Python packages and their versions needed by a project?', 'requirements.txt', 'index.html', 'style.css', 'config.ini for network only', 'A', 'requirements.txt declares dependencies so the project can be reproduced on other machines.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8585, 277, 'What is the main purpose of a README file in a project repository?', 'To explain what the project does and how to set it up', 'To store the database passwords', 'To replace the source code', 'To log compiler errors', 'A', 'A README introduces the project, its purpose and basic setup instructions for new users.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8586, 277, 'Which term describes a separate copy of code that can be developed independently and merged later?', 'A branch', 'A stack frame', 'A heap block', 'A network segment', 'A', 'Branches let developers work on features in isolation before merging changes back into the main line.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8587, 277, 'Which practice keeps each feature or fix small, tested and integrated frequently?', 'Continuous integration', 'Writing all code at the end', 'Never committing', 'Working without backups', 'A', 'Continuous integration merges small, frequent, tested changes to detect conflicts early.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8588, 277, 'Which term refers to writing code in small steps and checking it runs at each step?', 'Incremental development', 'Single-shot development', 'Deferred testing', 'Last-minute coding', 'A', 'Incremental development builds and verifies the system piece by piece, reducing risk.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8589, 277, 'What is a pull request used for in a typical Git workflow?', 'Proposing changes to be reviewed and merged', 'Deleting the repository', 'Rebooting the server', 'Creating a database backup', 'A', 'A pull request packages proposed changes for review, discussion and eventual merge.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8590, 277, 'Which tool is designed to manage the packages a project depends on in Python?', 'pip', 'git', 'docker compose', 'cron', 'A', 'pip installs and manages Python packages declared in a project''s requirements.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8591, 277, 'Two teammates edited the same file and now the merge reports conflicts. What is the correct next step?', 'Open the conflicted file, resolve the overlapping lines and commit the merge', 'Delete the repository and restart', 'Pick either version blindly', 'Ignore the conflict message', 'A', 'Merge conflicts must be resolved by editing the affected lines to a correct combined version before committing.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8592, 277, 'Which Git command records the current state of changes with a descriptive message?', 'git commit', 'git fetch', 'git remote', 'git rebase only', 'A', 'git commit saves the staged changes into the repository history with a message.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8593, 277, 'A feature branch has diverged from main. Which command integrates main''s changes into the feature branch?', 'git merge main or git rebase main', 'git push origin main', 'git clone', 'git status', 'A', 'Merging or rebasing the feature branch onto main brings in the latest shared changes to resolve divergence.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8594, 277, 'Which development practice most reduces the pain of a last-minute integration failure?', 'Integrating and testing changes several times per day', 'Waiting for the demo day', 'Coding in isolation for weeks', 'Disabling all tests', 'A', 'Frequent integration keeps the codebase in a working state and surfaces conflicts while they are still small.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8595, 277, 'A teammate reports the app crashes only on their machine. Which debugging step should come first?', 'Compare the environment, versions and configuration between machines', 'Reinstall the operating system', 'Rewrite the entire app', 'Blame the hardware', 'A', 'Environment differences such as dependency versions or OS settings are the most common cause of machine-specific crashes.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8596, 277, 'Which of the following is a defensive coding practice?', 'Validating inputs before processing them', 'Ignoring error returns', 'Using global variables everywhere', 'Hard-coding all credentials', 'A', 'Input validation rejects malformed or malicious data early, preventing crashes and vulnerabilities.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8597, 277, 'A reviewer sees the same block of code copied into ten places. Which refactor improves it?', 'Extract the repeated logic into a single reusable function', 'Copy it eleven times', 'Delete the file', 'Rename the variables randomly', 'A', 'Extracting duplicated logic into one function removes duplication and makes maintenance easier.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8598, 277, 'Which version control action uploads local commits to the shared remote repository?', 'git push', 'git clone', 'git diff', 'git stash', 'A', 'git push transfers local commits to the remote so teammates can pull and integrate them.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8599, 277, 'Which practice is most effective for ensuring new code does not break existing functionality?', 'Running an automated test suite on every change', 'Testing only on demo day', 'Reviewing nothing', 'Deleting old features', 'A', 'An automated test suite run on each change catches regressions immediately after integration.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8600, 277, 'A feature is complete but depends on a library version not yet approved. Which approach best reduces risk?', 'Pin the exact version, document it and review compatibility before merging', 'Upgrade every dependency blindly', 'Remove the dependency by rewriting everything', 'Ignore the issue', 'A', 'Pinning and reviewing dependency versions keeps the build reproducible and avoids surprise breakage.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1);
INSERT INTO questions (question_id, topic_id, question_text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, marks, question_type, is_active) VALUES
(8601, 277, 'A developer keeps committing generated build artifacts that cause noisy diffs. Which practice solves this cleanly?', 'Add the artifact paths to .gitignore and remove them from the repository', 'Commit them faster', 'Disable version control', 'Zip them into the source tree', 'A', 'Ignoring generated files keeps the repository clean and avoids meaningless merge conflicts.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8602, 277, 'Which Git workflow gives each feature its own branch, opens a pull request and requires reviews before merge?', 'GitHub Flow or a fork-and-branch workflow with mandatory reviews', 'Committing directly to main', 'No branching at all', 'Deleting branches after every commit', 'A', 'Branch-per-feature with reviewed pull requests protects the main branch and improves code quality.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8603, 277, 'A refactor changes internal APIs used by several modules. Which technique catches the breakage earliest?', 'Run the full automated test suite and fix failures before committing', 'Refactor without compiling', 'Announce the change on a poster', 'Ask users to adapt later', 'A', 'Running the complete test suite right after a refactor exposes broken callers while the change is fresh.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8604, 277, 'Which choice most improves the long-term maintainability of a capstone codebase?', 'Consistent naming, small functions and clear separation of responsibilities', 'One giant file with everything', 'No comments or structure', 'Copying code from random sources', 'A', 'Readable, well-structured code with clear responsibilities is far easier to debug and extend.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8605, 277, 'A critical bug found on Thursday must be fixed by Friday''s demo. Which approach is most responsible?', 'Fix it with a targeted patch, add a regression test and verify the fix', 'Remove the demo', 'Hide the bug', 'Rewrite the whole app overnight', 'A', 'A minimal fix verified by a regression test resolves the issue without introducing new risk.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8606, 277, 'Which practice best demonstrates that your implementation matches the planned architecture?', 'Maintaining a mapping from design components to actual modules in code', 'Deleting the design documents', 'Renaming modules after the demo', 'Hard-coding every decision', 'A', 'Traceability between design and code lets evaluators see the architecture was actually followed.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8607, 277, 'Why is automated testing valuable when many developers contribute to one capstone project?', 'It gives fast, consistent feedback that manual retesting cannot match', 'It removes the need for code review', 'It writes the features for you', 'It prevents all bugs', 'A', 'Automated tests give every contributor immediate feedback, keeping integration quality high as the team grows.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8608, 277, 'A teammate wants to merge a large change without any tests. Which professional action is best?', 'Require tests and review before merging the large change', 'Merge it instantly', 'Approve it without looking', 'Restart the repository', 'A', 'Demanding tests and review before merging big changes protects the shared codebase from regressions.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8609, 277, 'Which technique helps trace a subtle bug that only appears after the app has run for hours?', 'Add structured logging with timestamps and inspect the timeline', 'Reboot repeatedly', 'Remove all logging', 'Blindly change variables', 'A', 'Good logging reveals the sequence of events leading to the failure, making long-running bugs reproducible.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8610, 277, 'Which metric best indicates whether the team is developing at a steady, honest pace?', 'Velocity measured from completed, accepted features per iteration', 'Total lines of code written', 'Hours spent in meetings', 'Number of pull requests opened', 'A', 'Velocity from finished, accepted work reflects real progress better than code volume or activity counts.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8611, 278, 'Which level of testing verifies individual functions or components in isolation?', 'Unit testing', 'Load testing', 'Usability testing', 'Deployment testing', 'A', 'Unit tests exercise single functions or classes to confirm each piece works correctly on its own.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8612, 278, 'Which testing level checks that integrated modules work together?', 'Integration testing', 'Unit testing', 'Smoke testing only', 'Compile checking', 'A', 'Integration testing verifies that combined modules interact correctly through their interfaces.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8613, 278, 'What does a test case consist of in software testing?', 'A set of inputs, conditions and expected results used to validate a function', 'A compiler warning', 'A database table', 'A network cable', 'A', 'A test case defines specific inputs, preconditions and the expected outcome to validate behaviour.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8614, 278, 'Which tool is commonly used for writing unit tests in Python?', 'pytest or unittest', 'git', 'grep', 'curl', 'A', 'pytest and unittest provide frameworks to define, run and report unit tests in Python.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8615, 278, 'What does a regression test verify?', 'That previously fixed functionality still works after changes', 'That the network is fast', 'That the battery is charged', 'That the font is correct', 'A', 'Regression tests re-run past scenarios to ensure new changes did not break existing behaviour.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8616, 278, 'Which term describes testing done by the actual end users in a real environment?', 'Acceptance testing', 'Compiler testing', 'Static type checking', 'Linker validation', 'A', 'Acceptance testing lets users confirm the system meets their needs in realistic conditions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8617, 278, 'What does a code coverage metric measure?', 'The percentage of code executed by the test suite', 'The speed of compilation', 'The number of bugs hidden', 'The size of the repository', 'A', 'Coverage measures how much of the codebase is exercised by tests, highlighting untested paths.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8618, 278, 'Which test aims to verify that the most critical user journey works after a build?', 'Smoke testing', 'Alpha testing only', 'Penetration testing only', 'Database indexing', 'A', 'Smoke tests run the key paths quickly to catch build-breaking failures before deeper testing.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8619, 278, 'Which type of test checks how the system behaves under high user load?', 'Load or performance testing', 'Unit testing', 'Code style checking', 'Documentation review', 'A', 'Load testing measures response times and stability when many users access the system at once.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8620, 278, 'What is the primary goal of testing a capstone project before submission?', 'To find defects and verify the system meets its requirements', 'To make the demo longer', 'To consume extra time', 'To replace documentation', 'A', 'Testing exists to uncover defects and confirm that the implementation satisfies the stated requirements.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8621, 278, 'A test suite passes but a user still sees an error in the browser. What is the most likely gap?', 'Missing end-to-end or browser-level tests that cover the real user flow', 'The unit tests are too many', 'The code is too fast', 'The database is too big', 'A', 'Unit tests alone miss integration and browser issues, so end-to-end tests covering real flows are needed.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8622, 278, 'Which testing approach should you use when two modules exchange data through a JSON API?', 'Integration tests that call the real API and verify the exchanged payloads', 'Only UI colour checks', 'Only database backups', 'Only code comments', 'A', 'Integration testing exercises the actual API boundary, catching contract mismatches between modules.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8623, 278, 'Why is it important to write a test before fixing a bug?', 'The failing test reproduces the bug and later proves the fix', 'It makes the bug disappear', 'It avoids running the program', 'It deletes the code', 'A', 'A failing test captures the exact defect so the fix can be verified and future regressions caught.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8624, 278, 'A test depends on a live third-party service that is sometimes down. Which practice makes the tests reliable?', 'Mock or stub the external service so tests run deterministically', 'Run tests only on sunny days', 'Ignore the failures', 'Unplug the service', 'A', 'Mocking external dependencies makes tests fast and deterministic instead of flaky.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8625, 278, 'Which scenario best describes a boundary value test?', 'Testing values just inside and just outside a valid input range', 'Testing values chosen randomly', 'Testing only the middle value', 'Testing no inputs at all', 'A', 'Boundary value analysis focuses on edge cases at the edges of valid ranges where bugs cluster.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8626, 278, 'A feature passes all unit tests but is slow. Which test type should be added to quantify the problem?', 'Performance or timing tests that measure response times under load', 'More colour tests', 'More comment lines', 'More unit tests for speed of typing', 'A', 'Performance tests measure timing and throughput, exposing the slowness unit tests cannot detect.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8627, 278, 'Which step is essential when a test fails during a sprint?', 'Investigate the failure, decide if it is a code or test bug, and fix the root cause', 'Mark the test as ignored forever', 'Delete the test', 'Reboot the machine', 'A', 'Failing tests must be diagnosed to their root cause rather than disabled or deleted.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8628, 278, 'Which metric helps decide whether enough tests have been written?', 'Coverage plus review of the critical paths that remain untested', 'The number of test files alone', 'The size of the test folder', 'How tired the team is', 'A', 'Coverage combined with a review of important untested paths gives a balanced picture of test adequacy.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8629, 278, 'Which is the best order to run tests in a typical CI pipeline?', 'Fast unit tests first, then integration and end-to-end tests', 'End-to-end first, then unit tests', 'No tests at all', 'Tests after release', 'A', 'Running fast tests first gives quick feedback and catches most failures cheaply before slower tests run.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8630, 278, 'A change to a shared helper breaks three unrelated modules. What testing practice would have caught this?', 'A comprehensive regression suite covering all dependent modules', 'Testing only the helper', 'No tests', 'Testing only on demo day', 'A', 'A regression suite covering dependents catches the ripple effects of shared-code changes immediately.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8631, 278, 'A flaky test passes sometimes and fails sometimes with no code change. Which is the most professional response?', 'Reproduce it, find the nondeterminism such as timing or shared state, and make the test deterministic', 'Ignore it because it passes often', 'Delete it silently', 'Retry it in a loop', 'A', 'Flaky tests must be diagnosed to their source of nondeterminism so they give trustworthy signals.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8632, 278, 'Which testing strategy best balances speed and confidence for a capstone with limited time?', 'A test pyramid: many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests and a small set of end-to-end tests', 'Only end-to-end tests', 'Only unit tests', 'No tests because time is short', 'A', 'The test pyramid concentrates on fast, reliable unit tests with a smaller layer of slower end-to-end checks.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8633, 278, 'A security-sensitive feature stores user tokens. Which test type is essential in addition to functional tests?', 'Security tests that attempt token theft, injection and unauthorised access', 'Only layout tests', 'Only speed tests', 'Only backup tests', 'A', 'Security testing attacks the feature''s boundaries to reveal vulnerabilities functional tests miss.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8634, 278, 'Which practice proves the quality process itself was followed during the project?', 'Keeping test reports, coverage records and traceability from requirements to tests', 'Deleting test logs', 'Testing only in memory', 'Narrating tests verbally', 'A', 'Artifacts such as test reports and traceability matrices document that testing was systematic and verifiable.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8635, 278, 'Which defect-tracking data best helps the team decide what to fix before the demo?', 'Defects ranked by severity and impact on the demo-critical user journey', 'The total number of typos', 'The number of git branches', 'The font size of logs', 'A', 'Prioritising by severity and impact focuses scarce remaining time on what matters most to evaluators.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8636, 278, 'A heavy load test reveals the app crashes at 500 concurrent users although the target is 200. What is the correct conclusion?', 'The system meets its stated target and the crash point is above it, but a margin is still worth investigating', 'The app is perfect', 'Load testing is useless', 'The target must be raised to 500', 'A', 'Meeting the target with headroom is acceptable; the crash at 500 should still be logged and ideally improved.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8637, 278, 'Which technique isolates a bug that only appears when two test cases run together?', 'Check for shared mutable state and reset it between tests', 'Randomise the order', 'Run tests one by one forever', 'Increase parallelism blindly', 'A', 'Shared state leaking between tests is the usual cause; isolated, independent tests eliminate the interference.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8638, 278, 'Why is it valuable to automate regression tests rather than rely on manual retesting?', 'Automated suites run consistently and frequently, catching regressions far earlier', 'Manual tests are never wrong', 'Automation removes the need for design', 'Automation guarantees zero bugs', 'A', 'Automated regression suites give repeatable, fast feedback on every change, unlike manual spot checks.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8639, 278, 'A new developer asks which test to write first for an unfamiliar module. What is the best guidance?', 'Write a test for the module''s most important public behaviour and expected edge cases', 'Write a test for every private method', 'Write no tests', 'Copy tests from another project', 'A', 'Focusing on critical public behaviour and its edge cases gives the best protection per unit of effort.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8640, 278, 'Which outcome indicates the testing phase is genuinely complete rather than just time-expired?', 'All planned tests executed, critical defects resolved and coverage targets met', 'The calendar says the deadline passed', 'The team is tired', 'All tests are skipped', 'A', 'Completion is defined by executed tests, resolved critical defects and coverage targets, not by the clock.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8641, 279, 'Which activity makes a finished application ready for users to access?', 'Deployment', 'Compilation only', 'Code formatting', 'Password guessing', 'A', 'Deployment places the built application into a production environment where users can reach it.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8642, 279, 'Which tool packages an application and its dependencies into a portable unit?', 'Docker', 'Text editor', 'Spreadsheet', 'Calculator', 'A', 'Docker bundles the app and its dependencies into containers that run consistently anywhere.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8643, 279, 'What is a staging environment?', 'A replica of production used for final verification before release', 'The developer''s laptop', 'A test question bank', 'A backup tape', 'A', 'A staging environment mirrors production so releases can be verified before going live.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8644, 279, 'Which term describes the running application in the environment real users access?', 'Production', 'Development sandbox', 'Compiler cache', 'Source folder', 'A', 'Production is the live environment that serves the real user base.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8645, 279, 'What is the purpose of a live demonstration?', 'To show the working features of the project to evaluators or stakeholders', 'To hide the project', 'To compile the code', 'To format the drive', 'A', 'A demo walks through working features to prove the project''s functionality to the audience.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8646, 279, 'Which practice keeps application secrets out of the source code repository?', 'Using environment variables or a secrets manager', 'Writing them in comments', 'Storing them in public files', 'Emailing them to everyone', 'A', 'Environment variables and secret managers keep credentials outside the codebase where they could leak.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8647, 279, 'Which type of deployment releases the application to all users at once?', 'Big bang or rolling full deployment', 'Canary release', 'Blue-green swap', 'Shadow release', 'A', 'A big bang deployment replaces the old version with the new one for everyone simultaneously.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8648, 279, 'What is a rollback plan in deployment?', 'A defined procedure to return to the previous working version if a release fails', 'A plan to delete the project', 'A way to speed up the demo', 'A method to restart the laptop', 'A', 'A rollback plan restores the last known-good version quickly if the new release misbehaves.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8649, 279, 'Which file typically defines how a Docker container should be built?', 'Dockerfile', 'README.txt', 'style.css', 'database.sql', 'A', 'A Dockerfile contains the instructions for building a container image.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8650, 279, 'Which platform hosts containerised applications for easy deployment and scaling?', 'Kubernetes', 'Notepad', 'Paint', 'File Explorer', 'A', 'Kubernetes orchestrates containers, handling deployment, scaling and networking.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8651, 279, 'Which deployment strategy gradually shifts a small percentage of users to the new version to limit risk?', 'Canary release', 'Shutdown and restart', 'Uninstall everything', 'Delete the old version', 'A', 'A canary release exposes the new version to a small subset first, expanding only if it behaves well.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8652, 279, 'Which deployment strategy runs two identical environments and switches traffic between them instantly?', 'Blue-green deployment', 'Cold backup', 'Manual copying', 'Rebooting the server', 'A', 'Blue-green deployment keeps both an active and a standby environment, enabling instant, reversible switches.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8653, 279, 'A demo runs on the student''s laptop but fails on the evaluator''s machine. What is the likely cause?', 'Environment differences such as dependency versions or missing services', 'The evaluator''s screen size', 'The mouse brand', 'The room lighting', 'A', 'Environment drift is the classic reason demos fail on other machines; containers and documented setup prevent it.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8654, 279, 'Which practice best ensures the demo data does not contain real user information?', 'Using sanitised or synthetic demo data', 'Using real passwords on screen', 'Showing production logs', 'Streaming live user records', 'A', 'Synthetic or anonymised demo data avoids privacy issues while still demonstrating the features.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8655, 279, 'Which deployment artefact should be verified right before the final demo?', 'The exact build that will be shown, tested end to end in a clean environment', 'A build from six months ago', 'A screenshot of the code', 'A verbal description', 'A', 'Verifying the actual demo build in a clean environment prevents last-second surprises.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8656, 279, 'Which step is most important when deploying to a public cloud for the first time?', 'Securing access, configuring firewalls and keeping credentials secret', 'Disabling all security', 'Making the instance world-writable', 'Sharing the admin password', 'A', 'Initial cloud deployments must lock down access and protect credentials to avoid compromise.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8657, 279, 'A deployment pipeline uploads a new build, runs migrations and restarts services. What is this series of steps called?', 'A release pipeline or CI/CD workflow', 'A unit test', 'A code review', 'A commit message', 'A', 'CI/CD pipelines automate the build, test and release steps so deployments are repeatable.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8658, 279, 'Which approach minimises downtime when updating a database schema during deployment?', 'Backwards-compatible migrations that can run without breaking the old version', 'Dropping all tables first', 'Stopping the app forever', 'Deleting the database', 'A', 'Forward-compatible migrations let old and new versions coexist, keeping the app available during rollout.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8659, 279, 'What is the purpose of running a final dry-run demo for a teammate before the real one?', 'To catch technical glitches and rehearse the narrative', 'To hide the project', 'To waste time', 'To delete the build', 'A', 'A rehearsal surfaces glitches and smooths the presentation before the actual evaluation.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8660, 279, 'Which checklist item is essential before going live with a web app?', 'HTTPS enabled and a valid certificate', 'Removing all security', 'Using HTTP for everything', 'Sharing server credentials', 'A', 'HTTPS with a valid certificate protects traffic and is a baseline requirement for production web apps.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8661, 279, 'Which deployment technique lets you run the new version in production but route only test traffic to it first?', 'Shadow deployment', 'Deleting the old version', 'Shutting everything down', 'Restarting the router', 'A', 'Shadow deployment sends a copy of real traffic to the new version to validate behaviour without user impact.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8662, 279, 'A blue-green deployment fails after traffic switches. What is the fastest safe recovery?', 'Switch traffic back to the previous green environment using the prepared rollback', 'Rewrite the whole app', 'Delete the database', 'Reboot the office network', 'A', 'The standby environment enables instant rollback, which is exactly why blue-green is chosen.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8663, 279, 'Which deployment concern most affects a capstone that must keep running for the entire demo hour?', 'Stability and resource limits so the app does not crash or throttle mid-demo', 'The presenter''s voice', 'The background music', 'The projector model', 'A', 'Resource limits and stability matter because a crash mid-demo undermines the presentation.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8664, 279, 'Which practice lets a team prove deployment success objectively rather than by guesswork?', 'Health checks and metrics that confirm the service responds correctly', 'Fixing the screen brightness', 'Calling the demo ''good enough''', 'Disabling monitoring', 'A', 'Automated health checks and metrics verify a deployment actually works in the environment.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8665, 279, 'Why is a containerised deployment more reproducible than manual server setup?', 'The same image yields the same environment everywhere, removing configuration drift', 'Containers are always faster', 'Containers need no operating system', 'Containers hide all bugs', 'A', 'Reproducible container images eliminate environment drift between development and production.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8666, 279, 'A critical fix must reach production immediately without waiting for the next scheduled release. Which practice enables this?', 'A hotfix pipeline with automated tests that deploys the fix quickly and safely', 'Waiting a month', 'Emailing the database', 'Deleting the production instance', 'A', 'A hotfix pipeline lets urgent fixes go through a fast, tested path to production with controlled risk.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8667, 279, 'Which design makes a demo resilient to a failure in one backend component?', 'Graceful degradation and error handling so the app keeps working partially', 'Crashing with a blue screen', 'Hiding the error', 'Disconnecting the backend', 'A', 'Graceful degradation keeps the app usable when a component fails, making demos robust.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8668, 279, 'Which artefact most helps a future maintainer reproduce the production deployment?', 'Deployment scripts or IaC templates stored in the repository', 'A verbal description', 'A sticky note', 'A password list', 'A', 'Infrastructure-as-code and deployment scripts make the exact environment reproducible and auditable.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8669, 279, 'Why should the demo machine be prepared in advance with all services pre-started?', 'Avoiding cold-start delays and network timeouts that waste demo time', 'Because it looks professional', 'To drain the battery', 'To skip the presentation', 'A', 'Pre-warming services avoids slow cold starts and flaky network calls eating into the demo.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8670, 279, 'Which measurement tells you the deployed app actually serves users with acceptable performance?', 'Latency and error-rate metrics collected from the live environment', 'The number of commits', 'The font of the dashboard', 'The length of the code', 'A', 'Live latency and error-rate metrics reveal real user experience far better than static code measures.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8671, 280, 'Which session lets evaluators ask questions and verify the project in person?', 'Project defense or viva', 'A coffee break', 'A code review only', 'A social media post', 'A', 'A defense is a question-and-answer session where evaluators probe the team''s understanding of the project.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8672, 280, 'Which document gives a complete written account of the project''s work and outcomes?', 'Final project report', 'A text message', 'A receipt', 'A playlist', 'A', 'The final report documents objectives, methodology, results and conclusions of the project.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8673, 280, 'What is the typical purpose of the presentation slides in a project defense?', 'To summarise the project''s goals, approach and results clearly', 'To display all source code', 'To hide the project', 'To list personal hobbies', 'A', 'Slides structure the narrative of the defense, summarising what was built and why it matters.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8674, 280, 'Which section of a report states what the project set out to achieve?', 'Objectives or introduction', 'Bibliography only', 'Appendix of fonts', 'Back cover', 'A', 'The introduction and objectives section define the problem and the project''s intended achievements.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8675, 280, 'What is plagiarism in the context of a project report?', 'Presenting someone else''s work as your own', 'Using diagrams', 'Writing your own conclusions', 'Citing your sources', 'A', 'Plagiarism is taking others'' work without attribution, which is an academic offence.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8676, 280, 'Which report section lists all the books, papers and websites the project used?', 'References or bibliography', 'Table of contents', 'Index of figures', 'Glossary', 'A', 'The references section credits every external source cited in the report.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8677, 280, 'Which technique helps a presenter stay on time during the defense?', 'Rehearsing with a timer', 'Reading every slide verbatim', 'Speaking without a plan', 'Adding more slides at the last minute', 'A', 'Timed rehearsals help the presenter fit the narrative into the allotted slot.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8678, 280, 'What is the role of the project guide in the defense process?', 'To mentor the project and often sit in the evaluation panel', 'To write the report for the team', 'To present in place of the team', 'To grade only the slides', 'A', 'The guide mentors the team throughout and commonly participates in evaluation discussions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8679, 280, 'Which of the following is a key part of a good presentation opening?', 'A clear statement of the problem and the project''s aim', 'The full code listing', 'A random meme', 'Ten minutes of silence', 'A', 'A strong opening states the problem and aim so the audience knows the context immediately.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8680, 280, 'Which action is most appropriate if an evaluator asks a question you cannot answer?', 'Admit the limitation honestly and explain what you tried or would try', 'Fabricate an answer', 'Stay silent forever', 'Blame a teammate', 'A', 'Honesty about limitations is respected; guessing or blame damages credibility.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8681, 280, 'An evaluator asks how a particular design decision was made. Which answer is most convincing?', 'Explain the alternatives considered and the trade-offs that led to the choice', 'Say ''it just seemed right''', 'Change the topic', 'Read from the source code', 'A', 'Showing you weighed alternatives and trade-offs demonstrates genuine design thinking.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8682, 280, 'Which report section best demonstrates that you validated your solution against user needs?', 'Testing and evaluation results tied back to the requirements', 'The list of installed software', 'The word count', 'The cover page', 'A', 'Testing and evaluation sections show evidence that the solution meets its requirements.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8683, 280, 'Which presentation practice is most effective when discussing complex technical work?', 'Using simple diagrams and live demos to make the work tangible', 'Reading code aloud line by line', 'Using dense paragraphs', 'Skipping the details entirely', 'A', 'Diagrams and demos make technical concepts concrete and memorable for the audience.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8684, 280, 'A reviewer points out a flaw in your testing approach. What is the professional response?', 'Acknowledge it, explain any constraints and describe how you would improve it', 'Deny the flaw exists', 'Ignore the comment', 'Argue aggressively', 'A', 'Constructively acknowledging weaknesses and showing how to improve shows maturity and learning.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8685, 280, 'Which section of the final report should honestly discuss limitations of the work?', 'Conclusion and future work', 'The acknowledgements', 'The bibliography', 'The table of contents', 'A', 'Honest discussion of limitations and future work strengthens the report''s credibility.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8686, 280, 'Which practice helps the team handle tough questions during the viva?', 'Preparing anticipated questions and practising answers in advance', 'Avoiding eye contact', 'Speaking only about hobbies', 'Bringing no notes', 'A', 'Preparing for likely questions reduces surprise and improves calm, confident answers.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8687, 280, 'What is the main reason to include a system architecture diagram in the report?', 'It shows evaluators how the components fit together at a glance', 'It fills page space', 'It looks decorative', 'It replaces the abstract', 'A', 'An architecture diagram communicates the overall structure clearly and efficiently.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8688, 280, 'Which format best presents project results such as performance measurements?', 'Clear tables and charts with captions and interpretations', 'A wall of raw numbers', 'No results at all', 'Screenshots of errors', 'A', 'Tables and charts with interpretation make results understandable and persuasive.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8689, 280, 'An evaluator asks what you learned from the project. Which answer is strongest?', 'A concrete reflection on technical skills and project-management lessons', 'A list of course names', 'A random fact', 'A joke', 'A', 'Concrete reflections on real lessons show evaluators the experience had genuine value.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8690, 280, 'Which element makes a project demo compelling rather than merely functional?', 'A narrative that connects the feature being shown to the problem being solved', 'Rapid clicking without context', 'Showing only error messages', 'Skipping the demo entirely', 'A', 'A narrative linking features to the original problem makes the demo meaningful to the audience.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8691, 280, 'Which report practice best demonstrates that all claimed features are genuine?', 'Traceability between stated features, their implementation and the tests that verify them', 'Bold claims with no evidence', 'Screenshots of other apps', 'Vague descriptions', 'A', 'Traceability proves every claimed feature is implemented and verified, which is hard to fake.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8692, 280, 'An evaluator asks why the project''s approach is better than an existing open-source alternative. Which answer is strongest?', 'A comparison of measurable trade-offs such as accuracy, cost, latency and usability', 'A claim that it is better because we built it', 'A shrug', 'Silence', 'A', 'Concrete, measurable comparison with existing tools shows honest, informed evaluation.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8693, 280, 'Which defense strategy best handles a question that exposes a genuine project shortcoming?', 'Acknowledge it, relate it to documented limitations, and explain the planned future work', 'Invent a feature that fixes it', 'Deflect to another topic', 'End the presentation early', 'A', 'Owning a real shortcoming and linking it to planned improvements is far more credible than deflection.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8694, 280, 'What makes the conclusion of a final report persuasive to a technical evaluator?', 'It restates findings supported by evidence and ties them to the original objectives', 'It repeats the abstract word for word', 'It lists every minor task', 'It ends abruptly', 'A', 'An evidence-backed conclusion that returns to the objectives gives a strong, coherent finish.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8695, 280, 'Which citation practice protects the team from plagiarism accusations in a technical report?', 'Citing every external source with an accepted referencing style such as IEEE or APA', 'Copying text and adding a link', 'Naming sources verbally', 'Omitting all references', 'A', 'Consistent, standard citations properly attribute all external work, preventing plagiarism claims.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8696, 280, 'A teammate cannot attend the defense at the last minute. Which preparation makes the remaining team ready?', 'Every member rehearsed all sections and can cover the missing person''s part', 'Only one member knows anything', 'The slides are read-only for one person', 'The missing person was the only presenter', 'A', 'Cross-training all members on every section makes the team resilient to last-minute changes.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8697, 280, 'Which answer best demonstrates depth when an evaluator asks ''how would you scale this system?''', 'Discuss concrete bottlenecks such as database load and describe partitioning, caching or queues', 'Say it would never need scaling', 'Name a technology randomly', 'Decline to answer', 'A', 'Identifying real bottlenecks and proposing specific scaling techniques shows architectural understanding.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8698, 280, 'Why is a well-written executive summary valuable in the final report?', 'It lets busy evaluators grasp the project''s contribution quickly and accurately', 'It replaces the whole report', 'It is only for decoration', 'It hides the findings', 'A', 'An executive summary conveys the essence of the project at a glance, helping evaluators orient quickly.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8699, 280, 'Which practice most strengthens the credibility of measured results in the defense?', 'Explaining the methodology, sample size and conditions under which the results were obtained', 'Presenting results without any context', 'Rounding numbers up', 'Showing only the best run', 'A', 'Transparent methodology lets evaluators judge whether the measured results are trustworthy.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8700, 280, 'During the viva an evaluator challenges your choice of tech stack. Which reply shows the strongest reasoning?', 'Justify the choice against the project requirements, team skills and trade-offs measured during the project', 'Say the technology is popular', 'Blame the guide', 'Admit you never thought about it', 'A', 'Tying the stack to requirements and measured trade-offs shows deliberate, defensible engineering decisions.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8701, 281, 'What is professional ethics in the context of computing?', 'A set of moral principles guiding behaviour in the profession', 'A programming language', 'A type of database', 'A networking protocol', 'A', 'Professional ethics provides the moral principles that guide responsible behaviour in a profession.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8702, 281, 'Which principle means software professionals should avoid harming users through their work?', 'Do no harm', 'Always maximise profit', 'Never document code', 'Hide security flaws', 'A', 'Avoiding harm is a foundational duty of engineering and computing professionals.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8703, 281, 'Which of the following is a widely recognised code of ethics for computing professionals?', 'ACM Code of Ethics', 'A restaurant menu', 'A shipping manifest', 'A passport stamp', 'A', 'The ACM Code of Ethics outlines the responsibilities of computing professionals to society.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8704, 281, 'What does confidentiality mean in a professional context?', 'Protecting private information entrusted to you', 'Sharing passwords publicly', 'Copying files freely', 'Publishing all emails', 'A', 'Confidentiality obliges professionals to keep sensitive information private and secure.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8705, 281, 'What is a conflict of interest?', 'A situation where personal interests could influence professional judgement', 'A runtime error', 'A compiler warning', 'A DNS failure', 'A', 'A conflict of interest arises when personal or financial interests may bias professional decisions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8706, 281, 'Which behaviour is an example of professional integrity?', 'Being honest about what you have and have not done', 'Faking test results', 'Taking credit for others'' work', 'Hiding bugs', 'A', 'Integrity means honesty and consistency between one''s stated values and actual conduct.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8707, 281, 'Why should a professional stay updated with new technologies?', 'Competence requires keeping skills and knowledge current', 'It is required only on Fridays', 'To look busy', 'To avoid meetings', 'A', 'Maintaining competence means learning continuously because technology evolves quickly.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8708, 281, 'What does accountability mean for a software professional?', 'Accepting responsibility for the outcomes of your work', 'Deleting error logs', 'Blaming others', 'Ignoring failures', 'A', 'Accountability is owning the consequences, both good and bad, of your professional actions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8709, 281, 'Which action best reflects fairness toward colleagues and users?', 'Treating all people equitably and without discrimination', 'Rewarding only your friends', 'Withholding information', 'Overcharging clients', 'A', 'Fairness demands equitable, non-discriminatory treatment of everyone involved.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8710, 281, 'Which statement best describes a profession''s duty to society?', 'Weighing public interest alongside client and employer interests', 'Serving only yourself', 'Ignoring the public', 'Maximising personal gain only', 'A', 'Professionals have a duty to consider the public interest, not only their own or their employer''s benefit.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8711, 281, 'A colleague asks you to approve a test report that you know omits a real failure. What is the ethical action?', 'Refuse to sign it and report the omission honestly', 'Sign it to be helpful', 'Ignore the request', 'Hide the report', 'A', 'Approving a knowingly false report violates honesty and integrity, so it must be declined.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8712, 281, 'A manager pressures you to ship code with a known security vulnerability to meet a deadline. What should you do?', 'Refuse to ship knowingly unsafe code and raise the risk to the appropriate person', 'Ship it silently', 'Quit without discussion', 'Deny the vulnerability exists', 'A', 'Shipping knowingly unsafe code harms users, so the professional duty is to refuse and escalate.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8713, 281, 'A developer discovers they accidentally introduced a bug that briefly exposed user data. Which action is ethical?', 'Disclose the incident promptly and work to fix it', 'Delete the logs', 'Keep it secret', 'Blame the database', 'A', 'Prompt disclosure and remediation honour honesty and protect affected users.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8714, 281, 'Which situation is a clear conflict of interest for a software consultant?', 'Recommending a product from a company where they hold stock', 'Charging for extra work', 'Documenting decisions', 'Using open-source tools', 'A', 'Recommending products that enrich you personally compromises objective professional advice.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8715, 281, 'A team''s code passes tests but uses an accessibility-hostile design. Which ethical consideration applies?', 'Fairness and inclusion demand the system be usable by people with disabilities', 'Tests are the only concern', 'Accessibility is irrelevant', 'Users should adapt', 'A', 'Professional ethics includes fairness and inclusion, making accessibility a genuine ethical obligation.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8716, 281, 'Which response is most ethical when your employer asks you to collect user data without consent?', 'Explain the legal and ethical problems and refuse to proceed', 'Collect it because you are asked', 'Anonymise it then pretend', 'Charge extra for the data', 'A', 'Collecting data without consent breaches privacy ethics and law, so refusing and explaining is correct.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8717, 281, 'Which behaviour demonstrates honesty in reporting project progress?', 'Reporting real completion status even when it is behind schedule', 'Claiming the work is done before it is', 'Understating effort', 'Omitting stalled tasks', 'A', 'Accurate progress reporting lets stakeholders make informed decisions instead of being misled.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8718, 281, 'A free tool you built accidentally erases users'' files. What is the ethical first response?', 'Pause distribution, warn users and release a fixed version', 'Do nothing', 'Charge users for the fix', 'Deny the tool caused it', 'A', 'When software harms users, halting distribution, warning users and fixing it is the responsible course.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8719, 281, 'Which principle is most relevant when deciding whether to reuse code from another project?', 'Respecting licences and giving proper attribution', 'Ignoring licences', 'Copying without credit', 'Removing licence headers', 'A', 'Licences legally govern reuse, so respecting terms and attribution is an ethical and legal duty.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8720, 281, 'A new hire is asked to silently install surveillance software on office laptops. Which consideration matters most?', 'Whether the monitoring is lawful, disclosed and proportional', 'Whether it is easy to install', 'Whether it is cheap', 'Whether it is fast', 'A', 'Monitoring must be lawful, transparent and proportionate to be ethical.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8721, 281, 'Which action best models responsible disclosure when you find a serious vulnerability in a widely used product?', 'Notify the vendor privately, allow a fix window, then publish after coordination', 'Publish the exploit immediately', 'Sell the vulnerability silently', 'Keep it a secret forever', 'A', 'Coordinated disclosure gives vendors time to fix the flaw before details are made public.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8722, 281, 'An engineer is asked to write a review praising a product that actually underperforms. What is the ethical response?', 'Give an honest, evidence-based assessment', 'Write the praise to please the client', 'Refuse to engage at all', 'Exaggerate the numbers', 'A', 'Honest assessment protects the public even when the truth is inconvenient.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8723, 281, 'Which decision path best resolves an ethical dilemma involving conflicting duties to employer and users?', 'Weigh the risks to all stakeholders and escalate through the proper channels', 'Always obey the employer', 'Always favour the users blindly', 'Flip a coin', 'A', 'Stakeholder analysis and escalation help balance competing duties in an ethical dilemma.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8724, 281, 'A developer realises their algorithm disproportionately rejects qualified applicants from one demographic. Which duty applies?', 'The duty to avoid bias and to audit for fairness and transparency', 'The duty to deploy fast', 'The duty to hide the effect', 'The duty to ignore statistics', 'A', 'Fairness and transparency require auditing algorithms for discriminatory impact.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8725, 281, 'Which practice most strengthens ethical decision-making within a development team?', 'Creating a code of conduct and a confidential reporting channel', 'Never discussing ethics', 'Deciding issues privately', 'Punishing honest reports', 'A', 'Explicit codes and safe reporting channels institutionalise ethical behaviour.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8726, 281, 'An AI system gives no explanation for decisions that affect users. Which ethical principle is most compromised?', 'Transparency and explainability', 'Throughput', 'Latency', 'Aesthetics', 'A', 'Without explanations, users cannot understand or challenge decisions that affect them.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8727, 281, 'Which behaviour is most consistent with the principle of public good over personal gain?', 'Choosing to fix a dangerous bug that affects many users rather than a profitable pet feature', 'Prioritising the profitable feature', 'Ignoring the bug', 'Charging users for the fix', 'A', 'Serving the public good means prioritising harm reduction over personal profit.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8728, 281, 'A vendor offers you a gift to influence your choice of technology for a client. What is the ethical response?', 'Decline the gift and disclose the offer to your client', 'Accept it quietly', 'Accept it and choose the vendor', 'Accept it only if small', 'A', 'Declining and disclosing prevents undue influence and preserves professional independence.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8729, 281, 'Which approach best balances innovation with the ethical duty of care?', 'Building safety checks and impact assessments into the development process', 'Moving fast and ignoring risks', 'Deferring all safety', 'Hiding known issues', 'A', 'Embedding safety and impact review into development prevents harm without stopping innovation.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8730, 281, 'When asked to certify a product you did not fully test, what is the most professional response?', 'Decline to certify beyond the evidence and explain the gap', 'Certify it anyway', 'Certify with a false date', 'Decline and refuse all communication', 'A', 'Certification must match the evidence; certifying untested claims misleads the public.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8731, 282, 'Which type of IP protects inventions with a technical solution?', 'Patent', 'Trademark', 'Trade secret', 'Copyright', 'A', 'A patent grants exclusive rights over an invention that is novel, inventive and industrially applicable.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8732, 282, 'Which form of IP protects original creative works such as books, music and software?', 'Copyright', 'Patent', 'Trademark', 'Domain name', 'A', 'Copyright protects original expressions such as literary, musical and artistic works including code.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8733, 282, 'Which IP right protects brand names, logos and slogans that identify a business?', 'Trademark', 'Patent', 'Copyright', 'Trade secret', 'A', 'Trademarks distinguish the goods or services of one business from those of others.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8734, 282, 'Which type of IP protects confidential business information such as formulas and recipes?', 'Trade secret', 'Patent', 'Copyright', 'Geographical indication', 'A', 'Trade secrets protect confidential information that gives a business a competitive edge, like the Coca-Cola formula.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8735, 282, 'How long does copyright protection typically last for a work created by an individual?', 'The life of the author plus a set number of years (usually 50-70)', 'One year', 'Five minutes', 'Ten days', 'A', 'Copyright generally lasts for the author''s lifetime plus a statutory term, commonly 50 or 70 years.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8736, 282, 'Which of the following is NOT generally protected by copyright?', 'An idea or fact', 'A song', 'A book', 'A photograph', 'A', 'Copyright protects expressions, not ideas or facts themselves.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8737, 282, 'What does the term ''fair use'' or ''fair dealing'' generally permit?', 'Limited use of copyrighted work for purposes such as education or criticism', 'Unlimited copying for profit', 'Free redistribution of any software', 'Bypassing all rights', 'A', 'Fair use allows limited unlicensed uses such as criticism, comment, teaching and research under certain conditions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8738, 282, 'Which international convention harmonises patent protection across member countries?', 'The Paris Convention', 'The Geneva Accords', 'The Kyoto Protocol', 'The Helsinki Declaration', 'A', 'The Paris Convention established common rules for industrial property including patents across member states.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8739, 282, 'Which organisation administers international copyright treaties such as the Berne Convention?', 'WIPO', 'FIFA', 'ISO only', 'WTO only', 'A', 'The World Intellectual Property Organization (WIPO) administers treaties including the Berne Convention.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8740, 282, 'Which of the following can be patented?', 'A new, non-obvious and useful invention', 'A mathematical formula in isolation', 'A law of nature', 'A mere idea', 'A', 'Patents cover inventions that are new, non-obvious and useful; abstract ideas and laws of nature are excluded.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8741, 282, 'A student copies a diagram from a textbook into their report. Which practice keeps them legally safe?', 'Citing the source and relying on fair use for academic purposes', 'Copying without attribution', 'Scanning it upside down', 'Tracing it by hand', 'A', 'Proper citation plus fair use for academic purposes protects the student from infringement.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8742, 282, 'Which action would likely infringe the copyright of a software library?', 'Copying and redistributing the library''s code without respecting its licence', 'Reading its documentation', 'Installing it via a package manager', 'Calling its documented API', 'A', 'Redistribution beyond the licence''s terms is infringement; normal installation and API use are fine.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8743, 282, 'A startup uses a competitor''s unpatented formula they discovered independently. What is the legal situation?', 'They are free to use it because trade secrets protect only confidential misappropriation, not independent discovery', 'They must pay royalties', 'They must publish it', 'They must trademark it', 'A', 'Trade secret law protects against misappropriation, but independent development of the same information is lawful.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8744, 282, 'Which scenario describes patent infringement?', 'Making and selling a product that falls within the claims of an in-force patent', 'Reading a patent document', 'Attending a patent seminar', 'Publishing an article about a patent', 'A', 'Infringement occurs when someone makes, uses or sells a patented invention without authorisation.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8745, 282, 'A team finds an open-source library with a copyleft licence. What must they understand?', 'Derivative works must typically be released under the same licence', 'They can keep their code fully proprietary', 'The licence has no conditions', 'They must pay money', 'A', 'Copyleft licences such as GPL require derivative works to be distributed under compatible open terms.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8746, 282, 'Why do companies often rely on trade secrets instead of patents?', 'Trade secrets never expire and require no public disclosure', 'Trade secrets are always published', 'Patents last forever', 'Trade secrets are cheaper to register everywhere', 'A', 'Trade secrets remain protected indefinitely as long as they stay secret, whereas patents expire after disclosure.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8747, 282, 'A developer leaves a company with proprietary source code in memory. Which concern is most important?', 'They must not disclose or reuse trade secrets belonging to the former employer', 'They must delete their memories', 'They can publish the code freely', 'They must file a patent', 'A', 'Former employees are bound by confidentiality obligations not to disclose the employer''s trade secrets.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8748, 282, 'Which is the correct order for protecting an invention with a patent?', 'File a patent application with a description and claims, then get it examined and granted', 'Sell it first, then apply', 'Publish it first, then apply', 'Trademark it first, then apply', 'A', 'Patents require a complete application with claims that are examined before grant.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8749, 282, 'What does a trademark search help a new business avoid?', 'Accidentally choosing a mark that conflicts with an existing registered mark', 'Writing code', 'Hiring employees', 'Paying taxes', 'A', 'A trademark search reveals conflicting marks so the business avoids infringement and rebranding costs.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8750, 282, 'Which statement about software licensing is correct?', 'Licences grant specific rights and permissions; using software without the required licence may infringe copyright', 'Licences do not matter', 'All software is free', 'Licences only apply to hardware', 'A', 'Software is protected by copyright, and licences define the permitted uses.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8751, 282, 'A company reverse-engineers a rival''s product to build a compatible one. Which legal analysis matters most?', 'Whether reverse engineering breaches the licence, contract or relevant trade-secret laws', 'Whether the rival will notice', 'Whether the colours match', 'Whether the team is fast', 'A', 'Reverse engineering is evaluated against licence terms and trade-secret law, not just market behaviour.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8752, 282, 'A researcher combines two patented components into a new product. What must be assessed?', 'Whether the combination falls within the claims of existing patents and needs licences', 'Whether the parts are shiny', 'Whether the product is heavy', 'Whether the name is catchy', 'A', 'Combining patented parts can still infringe if the combination is covered by existing patent claims.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8753, 282, 'Which strategy best balances open-source contribution with protecting a company''s core advantage?', 'Publishing non-core modules openly while keeping differentiating algorithms as trade secrets', 'Publishing all code', 'Open-sourcing nothing', 'Ignoring licences', 'A', 'Strategic open sourcing builds goodwill while trade secrecy protects the real competitive edge.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8754, 282, 'Why do patents require an inventor to disclose the invention fully in the application?', 'The disclosure enriches public knowledge in exchange for the temporary monopoly', 'It speeds up the trademark office', 'It hides the invention', 'It prevents all competition forever', 'A', 'The patent bargain trades public disclosure for a limited exclusive right, advancing the public knowledge.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8755, 282, 'Which scenario most clearly constitutes trade-secret misappropriation?', 'An ex-employee shares a secret formula obtained under a signed NDA with a competitor', 'An employee learns general industry skills', 'A rival independently invents the formula', 'A journalist reports a public patent', 'A', 'Breaching an NDA by sharing the secret acquired in confidence is classic misappropriation.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8756, 282, 'A startup must choose between patenting and keeping a process secret. Which factor most favours a patent?', 'The process is easy to reverse-engineer from the final product', 'The process is impossible to detect', 'The process is a marketing phrase', 'The process changes every month', 'A', 'If rivals could reverse-engineer the innovation, a patent gives enforceable protection; otherwise secrecy may work.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8757, 282, 'Which analysis determines whether a new app infringes a competitor''s design patent?', 'Comparing the claimed design features of the patent with the app''s visual appearance', 'Comparing the app''s memory usage', 'Counting the app''s downloads', 'Reading the app''s reviews', 'A', 'Design patent infringement is judged by whether the accused design is substantially the same as the claimed design.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8758, 282, 'A company hires a developer who brings code written for their previous employer. Which practice is correct?', 'Ensure any reuse is licensed or rewritten, and respect the previous employer''s rights', 'Silently reuse it', 'Rename the variables', 'Publish the old employer''s code', 'A', 'Reusing a former employer''s code may violate copyright and confidentiality, so compliance must be ensured.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8759, 282, 'Which factor most determines the value of a patent portfolio for a technology company?', 'The breadth and enforceability of claims covering real products and competitors', 'The number of pages in each patent', 'The font of the documents', 'The colour of the cover', 'A', 'Claim breadth and enforceability over actual products drive a portfolio''s strategic value.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8760, 282, 'A team unknowingly infringes a patent held by a competitor. Which is the most responsible response?', 'Conduct a freedom-to-operate review, seek legal advice and adjust the design or licence', 'Ignore the patent', 'Stop all work', 'Sue first without analysis', 'A', 'A freedom-to-operate analysis identifies exposure early, allowing design changes or licensing before it grows costly.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8761, 283, 'Which law primarily governs cybercrime and electronic transactions in India?', 'The Information Technology Act, 2000', 'The Companies Act', 'The Motor Vehicles Act', 'The Indian Penal Code only', 'A', 'The IT Act, 2000 is India''s primary legislation covering electronic records, cybercrime and e-commerce.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8762, 283, 'What does GDPR stand for?', 'General Data Protection Regulation', 'Global Data Processing Rule', 'General Digital Privacy Right', 'Global Data Protection Registry', 'A', 'GDPR is the European Union''s data protection regulation governing personal data handling.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8763, 283, 'Which term describes personally identifiable information such as name, email or phone number?', 'Personal data', 'Metadata only', 'Compiled binaries', 'Network packets', 'A', 'Personal data is any information relating to an identified or identifiable person.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8764, 283, 'How is a data breach best defined in cyber security?', 'Unauthorised access to or disclosure of data', 'A scheduled backup', 'A software update', 'A password reset', 'A', 'A data breach is an incident where data is accessed or disclosed without authorisation.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8765, 283, 'Which principle of data protection means collecting only the data you actually need?', 'Data minimisation', 'Data duplication', 'Data hoarding', 'Data deletion', 'A', 'Data minimisation requires collecting and keeping the minimum amount of personal data needed.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8766, 283, 'What is informed consent in the context of personal data?', 'Permission given knowingly and freely after clear explanation of how data will be used', 'A signature obtained by trickery', 'Default opt-in with no explanation', 'Buying a data licence', 'A', 'Informed consent means users understand what they agree to and freely choose to permit it.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8767, 283, 'Which of the following is an example of sensitive personal data?', 'Health records', 'A shirt colour', 'A favourite movie', 'The weather', 'A', 'Health information is classified as sensitive personal data requiring heightened protection.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8768, 283, 'Which body typically handles complaints about data protection violations in the EU under GDPR?', 'A national data protection authority', 'The local fire department', 'The electricity board', 'The postal service', 'A', 'Each EU state has a data protection authority that supervises GDPR compliance and complaints.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8769, 283, 'Which act protects personal data in India and is modelled on GDPR principles?', 'The Digital Personal Data Protection Act', 'The Copyright Act', 'The RTI Act', 'The Competition Act', 'A', 'India''s Digital Personal Data Protection Act sets rules for processing personal data with consent and purpose limits.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8770, 283, 'Which right lets a person request that an organisation delete their personal data?', 'The right to erasure or right to be forgotten', 'The right to a free laptop', 'The right to skip work', 'The right to unlimited data', 'A', 'The right to erasure lets individuals ask organisations to delete their personal data under certain conditions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8771, 283, 'An app secretly shares user location with advertisers without disclosure. Which data-protection principle is violated?', 'Lawfulness, transparency and consent', 'Data speed', 'Data colour', 'Data volume', 'A', 'Processing without disclosure and consent breaches lawful, transparent and consent-based processing.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8772, 283, 'Which action does GDPR require when a serious personal-data breach occurs?', 'Notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours and affected individuals when risk is high', 'Delete all backups', 'Wait for a year', 'Never mention it', 'A', 'GDPR mandates prompt breach notification to the authority and, where risk is high, to affected people.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8773, 283, 'A company stores user data for marketing after the user cancels their account. Which right is relevant?', 'The right to erasure and objection to processing', 'The right to free shipping', 'The right to a refund in cash', 'The right to view advertisements', 'A', 'Former users can exercise erasure and objection rights to stop processing after the relationship ends.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8774, 283, 'Which practice best satisfies the GDPR principle of purpose limitation?', 'Using collected data only for the stated, documented purpose', 'Selling data to any buyer', 'Combining data for new hidden uses', 'Keeping data forever', 'A', 'Purpose limitation requires data be used only for the specific purpose for which it was collected.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8775, 283, 'What is the role of a Data Protection Officer (DPO)?', 'To oversee data protection compliance within an organisation', 'To delete all servers', 'To answer customer calls', 'To design marketing banners', 'A', 'A DPO monitors compliance, advises on data protection and liaises with supervisory authorities.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8776, 283, 'Which scenario most clearly triggers a GDPR breach notification?', 'A hacker exfiltrates customer personal data from the database', 'A font update fails', 'A printer jams', 'An email is misspelled', 'A', 'Unauthorised access to personal data is a reportable breach under GDPR.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8777, 283, 'Which of the following is a lawful basis for processing personal data under GDPR?', 'Consent, contract, legal obligation, legitimate interest, vital interest or public task', 'Being curious', 'Having the data', 'Paying for a licence', 'A', 'GDPR permits processing only under recognised lawful bases such as consent or legitimate interest.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8778, 283, 'A website''s cookie banner is designed to make accepting cookies easier than declining. Which principle does this undermine?', 'Freely given, informed consent', 'Fast loading', 'Good typography', 'Colour contrast', 'A', 'Manipulative design undermines the freeness and informed nature of consent required by privacy law.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8779, 283, 'Which measure best protects personal data stored in a cloud database?', 'Encryption at rest, access controls and regular audits', 'Leaving it unencrypted', 'Sharing the admin password', 'Storing a copy on a USB drive', 'A', 'Encryption, access control and audits are the core safeguards for stored personal data.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8780, 283, 'What does ''data portability'' allow a user to do under GDPR?', 'Receive their data in a structured, machine-readable format and transfer it', 'Duplicate their data into all databases', 'Buy additional data', 'Sell their data to competitors', 'A', 'Portability gives users their data in a usable format so they can move services.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8781, 283, 'A company processes personal data under ''legitimate interest'' but the user objects. What must the company demonstrate?', 'That its interests outweigh the user''s rights and freedoms, with a documented balancing test', 'That the user agreed in a past email', 'That it is profitable', 'That the user complained', 'A', 'Under legitimate interest, the controller must show the balancing test weighs in its favour when a user objects.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8782, 283, 'Which practice best enables GDPR compliance when sharing personal data with a third-country vendor?', 'Using adequacy decisions, standard contractual clauses or equivalent safeguards', 'Sending data without contracts', 'Hiding the transfer', 'Using unencrypted email', 'A', 'Cross-border transfers require approved safeguards such as SCCs to maintain GDPR-level protection.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8783, 283, 'Which design approach best satisfies privacy by design in a new app?', 'Building in data minimisation, access controls and user consent from the start', 'Adding privacy features after launch', 'Collecting everything first', 'Using default-on tracking', 'A', 'Privacy by design embeds protection into the product architecture rather than bolting it on later.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8784, 283, 'An employer monitors all employee communications without any policy or notice. Which concern is most serious?', 'The monitoring likely breaches data-protection law and employment-privacy rights', 'The emails are slow', 'The inbox is full', 'The calendar is crowded', 'A', 'Undisclosed, disproportionate monitoring violates data protection and privacy expectations.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8785, 283, 'Which measure most reduces the risk posed by a data breach if the database is stolen?', 'Encrypting the database and limiting what personal data is stored', 'Backing up the plaintext', 'Storing passwords in a text file', 'Making the database public', 'A', 'Encryption and minimisation make a stolen database far less dangerous to data subjects.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8786, 283, 'Why does GDPR treat biometric data with special care?', 'It is sensitive data whose misuse can uniquely and irreversibly identify and harm people', 'It looks interesting', 'It is easy to store', 'It has no legal meaning', 'A', 'Biometrics are irreplaceable identifiers, so GDPR classifies them as sensitive with strict safeguards.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8787, 283, 'Which of the following best supports a defensible data retention policy?', 'Documented retention periods tied to business need and legal requirements', 'Keeping everything forever', 'Deleting randomly', 'Storing all data in one place', 'A', 'Purpose-based retention schedules balance business need with legal and privacy obligations.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8788, 283, 'An AI system trains on scraped personal data without consent. Which principle is most at risk?', 'Lawful basis and fairness of processing', 'Processing speed', 'Model size', 'Deployment cost', 'A', 'Training on scraped data without a lawful basis undermines the fairness and lawfulness of processing.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8789, 283, 'Which organisational response best demonstrates accountability under GDPR?', 'Maintaining records of processing, doing privacy impact assessments and documenting decisions', 'Deleting all paperwork', 'Never logging data use', 'Relying on verbal promises', 'A', 'Accountability requires documented records, PIAs and decisions that prove compliance.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8790, 283, 'What is the maximum fine tier for serious GDPR violations?', 'Up to 4 percent of global annual turnover or 20 million euros, whichever is higher', 'A small warning', 'A refund of fees', 'A discount on licences', 'A', 'GDPR''s upper fine tier is 4 percent of global turnover or 20 million euros, whichever is greater.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8791, 284, 'What is corporate responsibility (CSR)?', 'A company''s duty to act ethically and consider social and environmental impacts', 'A marketing slogan', 'A tax reduction scheme', 'A building design', 'A', 'CSR means companies should behave ethically and contribute positively to society and the environment.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8792, 284, 'Which term describes the practice of reporting environmental, social and governance performance?', 'ESG reporting', 'Profit forecasting', 'Inventory counting', 'Payroll processing', 'A', 'ESG reporting discloses a company''s environmental, social and governance impacts to stakeholders.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8793, 284, 'What does corporate governance refer to?', 'The system by which companies are directed and controlled', 'The office furniture', 'The company logo', 'The annual party', 'A', 'Corporate governance is the framework of rules and practices guiding how a company is run.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8794, 284, 'Which stakeholder group directly owns a company?', 'Shareholders', 'The weather', 'Competitors only', 'The printer company', 'A', 'Shareholders hold ownership stakes in the company through their shares.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8795, 284, 'What is sustainability in a corporate context?', 'Meeting present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs', 'Maximising profits this quarter', 'Minimising salaries', 'Using unlimited resources', 'A', 'Sustainability balances economic, social and environmental needs for the long term.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8796, 284, 'Which of the following is an example of an environmental responsibility?', 'Reducing a company''s carbon footprint', 'Shortening meetings', 'Renaming products', 'Changing the logo colour', 'A', 'Lowering carbon emissions is a concrete environmental responsibility of organisations.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8797, 284, 'What is the purpose of a corporate code of conduct?', 'To set out the ethical standards expected of employees and leadership', 'To increase file size', 'To replace the constitution', 'To advertise products', 'A', 'A code of conduct formalises the ethical behaviour expected across the organisation.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8798, 284, 'Which group includes employees, customers, suppliers, community and shareholders?', 'Stakeholders', 'Programmers only', 'Auditors only', 'Competitors only', 'A', 'Stakeholders are all parties affected by or interested in a company''s activities.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8799, 284, 'What is the ''triple bottom line''?', 'Measuring profit, people and planet', 'Three bank accounts', 'Three product lines', 'Three factories', 'A', 'The triple bottom line evaluates a company''s economic, social and environmental performance.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8800, 284, 'Which of the following is an example of social responsibility by a company?', 'Running an ethical supply-chain programme that protects workers', 'Discarding old stock in a river', 'Exploiting cheap labour', 'Hiding safety risks', 'A', 'Protecting workers in the supply chain is a clear social responsibility action.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8801, 284, 'A company discovers its supplier uses child labour. Which action reflects genuine corporate responsibility?', 'Investigate, suspend the supplier and enforce corrective measures', 'Ignore it to keep costs low', 'Double the orders', 'Publicly deny it', 'A', 'Responsible companies act on supplier misconduct rather than look away for profit.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8802, 284, 'Which practice best demonstrates a company taking accountability for a product defect?', 'Issuing a recall, fixing the fault and communicating transparently', 'Hiding the recall', 'Blaming customers', 'Waiting for lawsuits', 'A', 'Proactive recall and transparency show genuine accountability to affected users.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8803, 284, 'A firm claims to be green but quietly ignores pollution at its factory. What is this called?', 'Greenwashing', 'Recycling', 'Carbon offsetting', 'Eco-labelling', 'A', 'Greenwashing is misleadingly presenting an environmentally responsible image while acting otherwise.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8804, 284, 'Which action best shows a company respecting its employees as stakeholders?', 'Providing fair wages, safe conditions and open communication', 'Paying minimum effort', 'Silencing feedback', 'Layoffs without notice', 'A', 'Fair treatment and safety are core expectations of employee stakeholder relations.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8805, 284, 'What is the main purpose of an independent board of directors in governance?', 'To oversee management and protect shareholder and stakeholder interests', 'To approve all salaries of competitors', 'To design ads', 'To file tax returns only', 'A', 'Independent boards provide oversight that checks management power and protects stakeholders.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8806, 284, 'Which of the following is an example of governance failure?', 'Managers falsifying financial statements to inflate share price', 'Holding an annual general meeting', 'Publishing annual reports', 'Electing an audit committee', 'A', 'Falsified accounts are a classic governance failure that misleads investors and regulators.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8807, 284, 'A company''s data breach affects millions of users. Which stakeholder communication is most responsible?', 'Prompt, honest disclosure with guidance for affected users', 'Silence', 'Denying it happened', 'Blaming the users', 'A', 'Honest, timely disclosure respects affected stakeholders and regulatory duties.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8808, 284, 'Which approach best reflects the stakeholder model of a company?', 'Balancing the interests of all affected groups rather than shareholders alone', 'Serving only shareholders', 'Ignoring all groups', 'Serving only management', 'A', 'The stakeholder model weighs the legitimate interests of every affected party.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8809, 284, 'Why do many companies publish annual sustainability reports?', 'To be transparent with stakeholders about environmental and social performance', 'To increase storage costs', 'To avoid audits', 'To advertise discounts', 'A', 'Sustainability reporting provides transparency and accountability to interested stakeholders.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8810, 284, 'A supplier''s factory has unsafe working conditions. Which action by the buyer company is most responsible?', 'Audit the factory, require corrective action and stop orders if needed', 'Keep ordering because it is cheap', 'Rename the supplier', 'Ignore the conditions', 'A', 'Auditing and enforcing improvement shows the buyer takes supply-chain responsibility seriously.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8811, 284, 'Which governance structure most reduces the risk of executives hiding misconduct?', 'An independent audit committee with whistle-blower protections', 'A single all-powerful CEO', 'No internal controls', 'Oral-only reporting', 'A', 'Independent oversight and safe reporting channels make misconduct harder to conceal.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8812, 284, 'A company must cut costs and considers closing a plant that employs an entire town. Which approach best balances responsibilities?', 'Engaging stakeholders, seeking alternatives and planning a responsible transition', 'Closing abruptly', 'Hiding the plan', 'Laying off everyone overnight', 'A', 'Responsible transition planning weighs community impact and stakeholder communication.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8813, 284, 'Which analysis framework helps a company evaluate its true social impact beyond profit?', 'Stakeholder and ESG impact assessments covering economic, social and environmental effects', 'A quick profit calc', 'A brand survey', 'A payroll check', 'A', 'ESG and stakeholder assessment give a complete picture of a company''s real impact.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8814, 284, 'A technology firm''s AI product is found to discriminate. Which action best reflects accountability?', 'Pause the product, fix the bias and disclose the findings', 'Deny the findings', 'Rename the model', 'Blame the users', 'A', 'Pausing, fixing and disclosing is genuine accountability for algorithmic harm.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8815, 284, 'Why might a company adopt an ethical supply-chain policy even when it raises costs?', 'It protects the brand, reduces legal risk and aligns with stakeholder expectations', 'It lowers quality', 'It is required by law everywhere', 'It reduces all costs', 'A', 'Ethical sourcing is a long-term investment in trust and risk reduction despite short-term cost.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8816, 284, 'Which practice best prevents corruption in corporate procurement?', 'Transparent bidding, conflict-of-interest declarations and independent audits', 'Secret deals', 'Personal favours', 'Cash payments', 'A', 'Transparency and independent oversight are the standard defences against procurement corruption.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8817, 284, 'A company quietly uses cheap components that fail after the warranty. Which stakeholder is most harmed?', 'Customers who depend on the product''s reliability', 'The marketing team', 'The office plants', 'The shareholders only', 'A', 'Hidden quality cuts harm customers who rely on the product, violating their trust.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8818, 284, 'Which reporting practice best demonstrates genuine commitment to sustainability rather than greenwashing?', 'Publishing verifiable targets, metrics and independent verification of results', 'Posting vague eco slogans', 'Framing one tree photo', 'Releasing no data', 'A', 'Measurable, verified targets separate real sustainability from image-driven claims.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8819, 284, 'When a company''s short-term profit conflicts with the community''s long-term wellbeing, what does good governance require?', 'Considering the wider impact and seeking a responsible balance with stakeholder input', 'Always choosing profit', 'Always choosing the community blindly', 'Deferring the decision forever', 'A', 'Good governance weighs long-term social impact against short-term gain through stakeholder engagement.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8820, 284, 'Which combination best builds a culture of corporate responsibility?', 'Leadership commitment, enforceable codes, incentives and transparent reporting', 'A single slogan', 'Fear of the CEO', 'Mandatory overtime', 'A', 'Responsibility is sustained by leadership, codes, incentives and transparency working together.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8821, 285, 'Which of the following is a classic case of corporate fraud involving falsified accounts?', 'Enron', 'A coffee machine', 'A traffic light', 'A pocket calculator', 'A', 'Enron is a landmark case of accounting fraud that collapsed after falsified financial reports were exposed.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8822, 285, 'Which case led to the Sarbanes-Oxley Act requiring stronger corporate governance?', 'Enron and WorldCom', 'The Merger of two car brands', 'A trademark dispute over a shoe', 'A minor fine for littering', 'A', 'The Enron and WorldCom scandals triggered SOX, which tightened financial reporting and accountability.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8823, 285, 'What is the main purpose of a professional code of conduct?', 'To set the standards of ethical behaviour for practitioners in a field', 'To increase licence fees', 'To replace the law', 'To decorate office walls', 'A', 'A code of conduct codifies the ethical standards professionals are expected to uphold.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8824, 285, 'Which of the following is a famous case about unauthorized data collection by a social network?', 'The Cambridge Analytica case involving Facebook data', 'The toaster recall', 'A parking ticket', 'A paint colour dispute', 'A', 'Cambridge Analytica is a widely studied case of unauthorised harvesting of personal data.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8825, 285, 'Which organisation maintains the Software Engineering Code of Ethics?', 'ACM and IEEE', 'The World Bank', 'UNESCO only', 'The Olympic Committee', 'A', 'The ACM and IEEE jointly maintain the Software Engineering Code of Ethics and Professional Practice.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8826, 285, 'What is the main ethical duty illustrated by the Volkswagen emissions scandal?', 'Avoiding deception about product performance', 'Designing sporty cars', 'Cutting production costs', 'Increasing advertising', 'A', 'Volkswagen deceived regulators and buyers about emissions, violating honesty and public trust.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8827, 285, 'Which term describes revealing wrongdoing inside an organisation to outsiders or authorities?', 'Whistle-blowing', 'Networking', 'Outsourcing', 'Benchmarking', 'A', 'Whistle-blowing is exposing misconduct that the organisation fails to correct internally.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8828, 285, 'In a code of ethics, what does ''public interest'' primarily require of professionals?', 'Prioritising the wellbeing of the public in their decisions', 'Maximising personal salary', 'Pleasing the loudest client', 'Avoiding all work', 'A', 'Public interest means professionals weigh the impact of their work on society as a whole.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8829, 285, 'Which of the following cases involved the theft of trade secrets by departing employees?', 'The Waymo vs Uber self-driving trade-secret lawsuit', 'A library fine', 'A rebranding of a soft drink', 'A stadium naming deal', 'A', 'Waymo accused Uber of using self-driving trade secrets taken by a departing engineer.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8830, 285, 'Which document typically defines the expectations of ethical behaviour for employees of a company?', 'Employee code of conduct', 'The product manual', 'The building lease', 'The parking policy only', 'A', 'The employee code of conduct sets out the ethical and behavioural expectations in the workplace.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8831, 285, 'In the Volkswagen case, which ethical failure was most central?', 'Engineers and managers colluded to defeat emissions testing', 'The cars were too fast', 'The logo was confusing', 'The dealerships were small', 'A', 'Deliberately circumventing emissions tests is deception of regulators and the public.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8832, 285, 'Which response is most ethical when you observe a colleague taking credit for another''s work?', 'Raise the issue through proper channels and support the wronged colleague', 'Stay silent', 'Also take credit', 'Post about it online', 'A', 'Addressing unfair credit through proper channels upholds fairness and integrity.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8833, 285, 'In the Cambridge Analytica case, what was the key ethical breach?', 'Personal data was harvested without users'' meaningful consent and used for targeting', 'The data was stored in the cloud', 'The software was written in Python', 'The ads were colourful', 'A', 'The core wrong was collecting personal data beyond users'' expectations and consent.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8834, 285, 'Which whistle-blowing scenario is most justified ethically?', 'Disclosing a genuine public-safety hazard after internal channels failed', 'Leaking private jokes', 'Revealing salaries out of spite', 'Posting trade secrets for fame', 'A', 'Disclosure is most justified when it prevents serious harm and internal remedies have been exhausted.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8835, 285, 'Which step should a professional take before publicly blowing the whistle on misconduct?', 'Report internally first and follow documented escalation paths', 'Go straight to the press', 'Tell everyone on social media', 'Remain silent forever', 'A', 'Internal reporting first respects the organisation''s processes and often resolves the issue without publicity.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8836, 285, 'In a case where a company faked test data, which principle did the engineers most directly violate?', 'Honesty and integrity in reporting results', 'Punctuality', 'Dress code', 'Team size', 'A', 'Falsifying test data is a direct violation of honesty and scientific integrity.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8837, 285, 'A code review finds a developer copied code without licence. Which action is most appropriate?', 'Remove or licence the code and notify the affected team', 'Leave it because it works', 'Hide it in another file', 'Blame the compiler', 'A', 'Unlicensed code is a legal and ethical problem that must be resolved promptly.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8838, 285, 'Which ethical principle is most relevant to the Theranos blood-testing scandal?', 'The duty not to deceive patients about the validity of medical results', 'The duty to raise prices', 'The duty to brand well', 'The duty to hire many people', 'A', 'Theranos misled patients with unreliable results, violating honesty and safety duties.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8839, 285, 'What is the purpose of ethics case studies in professional training?', 'To practise reasoning about realistic dilemmas before facing them', 'To memorise company names', 'To learn marketing', 'To earn certificates faster', 'A', 'Case studies let professionals rehearse ethical reasoning in realistic, low-stakes situations.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8840, 285, 'Which of the following best describes the ethical lesson of the Theranos scandal?', 'Claims about product performance must be validated by independent, honest testing', 'Marketing must be loud', 'Investors should never ask questions', 'Testing is optional', 'A', 'Theranos shows that unverified performance claims can cause real harm and must be independently validated.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8841, 285, 'In the Enron case, what role did auditors play in the ethical failure?', 'They failed to question irregular accounting, undermining their independence', 'They improved the software', 'They designed the building', 'They set the share price', 'A', 'Compromised auditor independence allowed the fraud to go undetected, a key governance lesson.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8842, 285, 'Which governance change directly resulted from the Enron and WorldCom scandals?', 'The Sarbanes-Oxley Act requiring internal controls and executive certification', 'A ban on all mergers', 'Mandatory open offices', 'A freeze on executive pay forever', 'A', 'SOX mandated stronger internal controls, independent audits and CEO certification of financial statements.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8843, 285, 'Which ethical framework best explains the decision to recall a dangerous product proactively?', 'Utilitarianism, which weighs overall public benefit against the company''s cost', 'A pure profit test', 'A marketing plan', 'A personal preference', 'A', 'A recall prioritises the greatest good for the many, a hallmark of utilitarian reasoning.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8844, 285, 'Which analysis distinguishes a justified whistle-blower from a malicious leaker?', 'The severity of the harm, the motive and whether internal channels were tried', 'The leaker''s job title', 'The length of the document', 'The day of the week', 'A', 'Justified whistle-blowing is driven by harm prevention, honest motive and exhausted internal remedies.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8845, 285, 'In the Waymo vs Uber case, which process failure enabled the alleged trade-secret theft?', 'Weak onboarding and due-diligence controls when the engineer was hired', 'Too many meetings', 'Fast cars', 'Bright logos', 'A', 'The case highlighted how weak hiring controls let protected material move between companies.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8846, 285, 'Which response best demonstrates applying a code of ethics to an ambiguous real-world dilemma?', 'Document the dilemma, consult the code and seek guidance from peers or ethics bodies', 'Flip a coin', 'Follow the loudest opinion', 'Ignore the issue', 'A', 'Structured consultation with the code and experienced peers yields defensible ethical decisions.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8847, 285, 'Which ethical lesson is most directly drawn from the Cambridge Analytica case for developers?', 'Data collection design must respect consent even when profitable', 'APIs should be faster', 'Ads should be bigger', 'Users are responsible for everything', 'A', 'The case taught that technical convenience must never override user consent and privacy.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8848, 285, 'When two code-of-conduct clauses conflict, which method best resolves the dilemma?', 'Weighing the underlying values such as public safety against confidentiality case by case', 'Following the alphabet', 'Choosing randomly', 'Ignoring the conflict', 'A', 'Ethical reasoning ranks underlying values to resolve conflicts between competing clauses.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8849, 285, 'Which outcome of the Theranos case best illustrates corporate accountability?', 'Executives faced criminal charges and the company collapsed after the fraud was exposed', 'The company won an award', 'The product was renamed', 'The investors apologised', 'A', 'Criminal accountability and collapse show that sustained deception has serious consequences.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8850, 285, 'Which case most clearly demonstrates the ethical duty of transparency in AI deployment?', 'The rejection of automated decision tools that could not be explained to those affected', 'A faster sorting algorithm', 'A new colour scheme', 'A smaller server', 'A', 'Demands for explainable, transparent AI decisions stem from the ethical duty to not harm users invisibly.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1);
INSERT INTO questions (question_id, topic_id, question_text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, marks, question_type, is_active) VALUES
(8851, 296, 'What is the basic unit of information in quantum computing?', 'Qubit', 'Bit', 'Byte', 'Nibble', 'A', 'A qubit (quantum bit) is the fundamental unit of quantum information.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8852, 296, 'Which quantum phenomenon lets a qubit represent 0 and 1 at the same time?', 'Superposition', 'Gravity', 'Entropy', 'Friction', 'A', 'Superposition allows a qubit to exist in a combination of 0 and 1 states simultaneously.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8853, 296, 'Which quantum phenomenon links the states of qubits so measuring one affects another?', 'Entanglement', 'Momentum', 'Diffraction', 'Refraction', 'A', 'Entanglement correlates qubits so their states are linked even across distance.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8854, 296, 'What is the classical equivalent of a qubit?', 'A bit', 'A register', 'A cache line', 'A sector', 'A', 'A classical bit is the standard unit of information, holding either 0 or 1.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8855, 296, 'Which fundamental building block performs operations on qubits?', 'Quantum gates', 'Resistors', 'Capacitors', 'Gears', 'A', 'Quantum gates transform qubit states, forming quantum circuits.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8856, 296, 'What happens to a qubit''s superposition when it is measured?', 'It collapses to a definite 0 or 1', 'It stays in superposition', 'It disappears', 'It doubles', 'A', 'Measurement collapses a qubit''s superposition into a definite classical outcome.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8857, 296, 'Which of the following is a potential application of quantum computing?', 'Factoring large numbers for cryptography', 'Printing documents', 'Formatting text', 'Serving static web pages', 'A', 'Quantum algorithms such as Shor''s can factor large numbers exponentially faster than classical ones.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8858, 296, 'What does a classical bit store?', 'A single 0 or 1', 'Both 0 and 1', 'Any real number', 'A vector of states', 'A', 'A classical bit is binary, holding exactly one of two values.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8859, 296, 'Which company and IBM-like ecosystem approach runs quantum processors via the cloud?', 'Providing remote access to real quantum hardware', 'Selling quantum PCs', 'Replacing the internet', 'Emulating only', 'A', 'Cloud quantum services let researchers run circuits on real quantum hardware remotely.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8860, 296, 'Which property of quantum computers makes them difficult to build?', 'Extreme sensitivity to noise and environmental interference', 'Too much memory', 'Overheating of screens', 'Poor keyboard design', 'A', 'Qubits decohere easily, so maintaining quantum states requires extreme isolation.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8861, 296, 'Which famous quantum algorithm can factor large integers efficiently?', 'Shor''s algorithm', 'Dijkstra''s algorithm', 'Quick sort', 'Binary search', 'A', 'Shor''s algorithm factors integers in polynomial time, threatening RSA-based security.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8862, 296, 'Which quantum algorithm provides a quadratic speedup for unstructured search?', 'Grover''s algorithm', 'Linear search', 'Bubble sort', 'Euclid''s algorithm', 'A', 'Grover''s algorithm searches an unsorted database in roughly the square root of classical time.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8863, 296, 'Which problem does quantum key distribution (QKD) address?', 'Detecting eavesdropping during key exchange', 'Speeding up file compression', 'Improving screen resolution', 'Reducing power use', 'A', 'QKD exploits measurement disturbance so any interception is detectable.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8864, 296, 'Which concept describes the probability distribution of a qubit''s measurement outcomes?', 'Born rule probabilities from amplitudes', 'The file size', 'The clock speed', 'The cache size', 'A', 'The Born rule derives outcome probabilities from the squared amplitudes of the qubit state.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8865, 296, 'Which of the following best describes quantum decoherence?', 'Loss of quantum behaviour due to interaction with the environment', 'A gain in processing speed', 'An increase in cache', 'A screen refresh', 'A', 'Decoherence destroys superpositions through unwanted environmental interaction, causing errors.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8866, 296, 'Which class of quantum errors is addressed by quantum error correction?', 'Bit flips and phase flips on qubits', 'Disk fragmentation', 'Network congestion', 'Battery drain', 'A', 'Quantum error correction encodes logical qubits to detect and correct bit and phase errors.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8867, 296, 'Which gate creates superposition by putting a qubit into an equal mix of 0 and 1?', 'The Hadamard (H) gate', 'The NOT gate', 'The identity gate', 'The swap gate', 'A', 'The Hadamard gate creates an equal superposition of basis states.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8868, 296, 'What is the main current limitation of ''noisy intermediate-scale quantum'' (NISQ) devices?', 'High error rates that limit circuit depth', 'Too much accuracy', 'Excessive speed', 'Unlimited qubits', 'A', 'NISQ devices have noisy qubits, so practical circuits must stay short and shallow.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8869, 296, 'Which pair correctly matches a quantum concept to its classical analogue?', 'Superposition to holding both possibilities simultaneously', 'Entanglement to a single bit', 'Qubit to a whole register', 'Gate to a database', 'A', 'Superposition is the quantum analogue of holding multiple possibilities at once, unlike classical states.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8870, 296, 'Which cryptographic system is considered most vulnerable to a future large-scale quantum computer?', 'RSA based on integer factoring', 'One-time pads with secure keys', 'Post-quantum lattice ciphers', 'AES with a truly random key', 'A', 'Shor''s algorithm would break RSA by factoring its large modulus efficiently.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8871, 296, 'Which strategy is most appropriate for migrating cryptography before quantum computers mature?', 'Adopting post-quantum (lattice-based) algorithms and hybrid encryption now', 'Waiting until quantum machines are built', 'Ignoring the risk', 'Doubling key length only', 'A', 'Post-quantum and hybrid schemes provide forward security against harvest-now-decrypt-later attacks.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8872, 296, 'Which research challenge must be solved before fault-tolerant quantum computers become practical?', 'Scaling error-corrected logical qubits beyond noisy physical qubits', 'Building larger monitors', 'Writing longer programs', 'Improving keyboard latency', 'A', 'The gap between physical and fault-tolerant logical qubits is the central engineering challenge.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8873, 296, 'Which of the following is a promising application where quantum advantage may first appear?', 'Simulating molecular systems for chemistry and materials', 'Rendering simple text', 'Counting clicks', 'Sorting small arrays', 'A', 'Quantum simulation of molecules is expected to outperform classical computers for chemistry.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8874, 296, 'Which threat does ''harvest now, decrypt later'' pose to current encrypted data?', 'Adversaries store ciphertext today and decrypt it once quantum computers exist', 'Encryption becomes slower today', 'Data disappears now', 'Keys grow automatically', 'A', 'Encrypted data captured today may be decrypted later, so post-quantum readiness is urgent.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8875, 296, 'Which trade-off must a quantum algorithm designer manage?', 'Circuit depth and error rate versus the speedup achieved', 'Screen brightness versus battery', 'File size versus font', 'Bandwidth versus pixels', 'A', 'Deep circuits on noisy hardware accumulate errors, limiting the achievable speedup.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8876, 296, 'Which technique measures how close a quantum circuit''s output is to the ideal result?', 'Fidelity comparison against the ideal state', 'A disk check', 'A speed test', 'A colour histogram', 'A', 'Fidelity quantifies the correctness of quantum computation relative to the ideal output.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8877, 296, 'Why is quantum key distribution considered information-theoretically secure?', 'Any measurement of the quantum key is detectable by the legitimate parties', 'It uses longer passwords', 'It relies on secret algorithms', 'It encrypts twice', 'A', 'The laws of quantum measurement guarantee that eavesdropping disturbs the key and is detected.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8878, 296, 'Which approach reduces quantum errors without requiring fully fault-tolerant hardware?', 'Mitigating errors with post-processing and calibration in NISQ systems', 'Buying more qubits', 'Increasing clock speed', 'Cooling the console', 'A', 'Error mitigation corrects noisy results statistically, improving near-term usefulness.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8879, 296, 'Which discipline studies algorithms designed to exploit quantum superposition and entanglement?', 'Quantum algorithm theory', 'Database normalisation', 'Network routing', 'Compiler optimisation', 'A', 'Quantum algorithm theory designs algorithms that leverage quantum effects for speedups.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8880, 296, 'Which research question best frames a thesis in quantum software rather than hardware?', 'How to compile and optimise quantum circuits for noisy devices', 'How to build a better screen', 'How to cool a laptop', 'How to wire a router', 'A', 'Quantum software research focuses on compilers, optimisation and error handling for real devices.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8881, 297, 'What is edge AI?', 'Running AI models on devices near the data source instead of in the cloud', 'Training models only in a data center', 'Printing results', 'Manual data entry', 'A', 'Edge AI executes inference close to where data is generated, reducing latency.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8882, 297, 'What does TinyML refer to?', 'Running small machine learning models on microcontrollers', 'Very large cloud models', 'Statistical spreadsheets', 'Database backups', 'A', 'TinyML brings ML to low-power embedded devices such as microcontrollers.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8883, 297, 'Which step happens when a trained model makes a prediction on a device?', 'Inference', 'Backpropagation only', 'Data entry', 'Recompilation', 'A', 'Inference is the process of running a trained model to produce predictions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8884, 297, 'Which benefit does on-device inference provide over cloud inference?', 'Lower latency because data does not travel to the cloud', 'Larger model size', 'Guaranteed internet dependency', 'Higher power consumption', 'A', 'On-device inference avoids network round trips, cutting response time.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8885, 297, 'Which concern is reduced when private data stays on the device?', 'Privacy exposure during transmission', 'Model accuracy', 'Battery usage', 'Storage cost', 'A', 'Keeping data on-device avoids sending sensitive information across the network.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8886, 297, 'Which of the following is a typical device for edge inference?', 'A smartphone', 'A mainframe', 'A cloud VM', 'A large warehouse server', 'A', 'Smartphones commonly run lightweight on-device models for features like face unlock.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8887, 297, 'Which technique shrinks a large model so it fits on a small device?', 'Model compression such as pruning and quantisation', 'Adding more layers', 'Increasing precision', 'Training longer', 'A', 'Pruning, quantisation and distillation reduce model size for edge deployment.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8888, 297, 'What is quantisation in edge ML?', 'Reducing the numeric precision of model weights', 'Increasing the data rate', 'Adding more parameters', 'Duplicating the model', 'A', 'Quantisation converts weights to lower precision, saving memory and compute.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8889, 297, 'Which of the following is an example of an edge AI application?', 'Voice assistants responding on the phone', 'A nightly cloud batch job', 'A paper ledger', 'An email newsletter', 'A', 'On-device voice assistants process speech locally for instant response.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8890, 297, 'Which framework is designed specifically for on-device ML?', 'TensorFlow Lite', 'A word processor', 'A spreadsheet app', 'A web server', 'A', 'TensorFlow Lite is optimised for running models on mobile and embedded devices.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8891, 297, 'A fitness watch must detect falls instantly even without internet. Which approach fits best?', 'Running a lightweight fall-detection model on the watch itself', 'Sending video to the cloud', 'Waiting for Wi-Fi', 'Manual checking', 'A', 'On-device inference lets the watch react instantly and offline.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8892, 297, 'Which trade-off dominates when deploying ML on a microcontroller?', 'Model accuracy versus limited memory, compute and battery', 'Internet bandwidth', 'Screen resolution', 'Keyboard size', 'A', 'Embedded devices force a balance between accuracy and scarce resources.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8893, 297, 'Which technique adapts a cloud-trained model to work well on diverse edge devices?', 'Federated learning that trains across devices without sharing raw data', 'Sending all data to one server', 'Using a larger cloud model', 'Disabling updates', 'A', 'Federated learning improves models using device data while keeping data local.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8894, 297, 'Which approach reduces the battery cost of always-on edge models?', 'Duty cycling and wake-on-event inference', 'Running models constantly at full power', 'Increasing sampling rate', 'Using cloud models always', 'A', 'Event-triggered inference and duty cycling keep power consumption low.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8895, 297, 'Which problem does model distillation solve for edge deployment?', 'Training a small model to mimic a large teacher model', 'Increasing the teacher''s size', 'Slowing inference', 'Doubling memory use', 'A', 'Distillation transfers knowledge from a large model to a compact student.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8896, 297, 'Which concern most affects on-device model updates in the field?', 'Rolling out new model versions reliably across many devices', 'The office temperature', 'The colour of the app', 'The screen protector', 'A', 'Device fleets need reliable, staged rollout of updated models.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8897, 297, 'Which hardware component on a phone accelerates neural network inference?', 'A dedicated NPU or GPU', 'The SIM card', 'The charger', 'The microphone', 'A', 'Neural processing units and GPUs accelerate on-device neural inference.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8898, 297, 'Which technique lets a single edge model handle multiple tasks efficiently?', 'Multitask learning that shares features across tasks', 'Training one model per task with full duplication', 'Ignoring extra tasks', 'Loading all models at once', 'A', 'Multitask learning shares computation, saving memory and power.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8899, 297, 'Which scenario most benefits from hybrid edge-cloud AI?', 'Lightweight edge processing with heavy tasks offloaded to the cloud', 'Sending every pixel to the cloud', 'No cloud at all', 'No edge at all', 'A', 'Hybrid designs balance instant local responses with the cloud''s larger models.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8900, 297, 'Which design best minimises energy while keeping an always-listening assistant responsive?', 'A hierarchical pipeline from tiny wake-word model to full model on demand', 'Running the full model continuously', 'Disabling the assistant', 'Polling the cloud every second', 'A', 'A tiny wake-word detector activates the full model only when needed.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8901, 297, 'Which concern is critical when deploying a medical alert model on a patient''s watch?', 'Validating accuracy on real-world device data and handling edge cases', 'The watch face colour', 'The band material', 'The screen brightness', 'A', 'Medical alert models must be validated on realistic device data to avoid dangerous failures.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8902, 297, 'Which approach best preserves privacy when training a shared model across many phones?', 'Federated learning that aggregates updates without collecting raw data', 'Uploading all photos to a server', 'Storing models in the cloud only', 'Training on one device', 'A', 'Federated learning trains on local data and shares only model updates, protecting privacy.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8903, 297, 'Which trade-off does an engineer face when quantising a model for a smart camera?', 'Smaller size and speed versus a slight loss in accuracy', 'More accuracy and more memory', 'No trade-off', 'Better images and slower frames', 'A', 'Quantisation trades minor accuracy loss for significant size and speed gains.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8904, 297, 'Which design decision most affects the battery life of an always-on vision model?', 'Choosing between continuous processing and event-triggered inference', 'The camera brand', 'The app icon', 'The screen lock type', 'A', 'Event-triggered inference keeps the model dormant until motion or a trigger occurs.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8905, 297, 'Which strategy best reduces the carbon and bandwidth cost of edge model training?', 'Distributed on-device training with periodic small updates', 'Uploading the full dataset repeatedly', 'Training a new model from scratch in the cloud', 'Sending all data daily', 'A', 'On-device learning with sparse updates cuts bandwidth and energy compared with full data uploads.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8906, 297, 'Which problem does on-device personalisation most directly solve?', 'Tailoring predictions to an individual user''s private local data', 'Reducing model variety', 'Slowing inference', 'Removing privacy features', 'A', 'On-device personalisation adapts models to each user while keeping their data private.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8907, 297, 'Which security concern is unique to edge AI deployments?', 'Models and data on devices being extracted or tampered with by attackers', 'The office network speed', 'The printer ink', 'The monitor size', 'A', 'Physical access to devices exposes models and data to extraction, requiring hardening.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8908, 297, 'Which approach best balances edge AI benefits with cloud model quality?', 'Cascading edge decisions to the cloud only for low-confidence cases', 'Sending everything to the cloud', 'Never using the cloud', 'Disabling edge models', 'A', 'Cascading designs get instant edge responses while preserving cloud accuracy for hard cases.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8909, 297, 'Which metric best captures the energy cost of an edge inference?', 'Joules per inference on the target hardware', 'Frames per second only', 'Model layers', 'Parameters count', 'A', 'Energy per inference directly measures battery impact on edge devices.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8910, 297, 'Which validation approach best ensures an edge model stays fair across user populations?', 'Evaluating performance across demographic and device subgroups during testing', 'Testing on one group only', 'Ignoring fairness', 'Using only average accuracy', 'A', 'Subgroup evaluation catches biases that average accuracy hides.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8911, 298, 'Which term refers to overlaying digital content onto the real world?', 'Augmented Reality', 'Automated Routing', 'Abstract Rendering', 'Applied Robotics', 'A', 'Augmented reality overlays digital content onto the real world.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8912, 298, 'Which term describes an immersive, fully synthetic environment?', 'Virtual Reality', 'Verified Rendering', 'Visual Registry', 'Variable Response', 'A', 'Virtual reality immerses the user in a fully synthetic environment.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8913, 298, 'Which technology blends real and virtual worlds interactively?', 'Mixed reality (MR)', 'A spreadsheet', 'A printer', 'A text editor', 'A', 'Mixed reality merges physical and digital worlds with real-time interaction.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8914, 298, 'Which device displays VR content close to the user''s eyes?', 'A head-mounted display (HMD)', 'A projector on a wall', 'A laptop screen', 'A smartwatch', 'A', 'HMDs place displays directly in front of the eyes to create immersion.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8915, 298, 'Which of the following best describes the metaverse?', 'A collective virtual shared space built from interconnected digital worlds', 'A single mobile game', 'A chat app', 'A website', 'A', 'The metaverse is an interoperable network of persistent virtual worlds.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8916, 298, 'Which of the following is a key hardware input for VR interaction?', 'Motion controllers', 'A mouse', 'A keyboard', 'A scanner', 'A', 'Motion controllers track hands for natural interaction in VR.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8917, 298, 'Which field does AR most commonly enhance on smartphones?', 'Navigation and camera overlays', 'Text compression', 'Battery management', 'File compression', 'A', 'AR adds digital directions and information to live camera views.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8918, 298, 'What does presence mean in VR?', 'The feeling of being inside the virtual environment', 'Being logged in', 'Having a seat', 'A network signal', 'A', 'Presence is the sense of truly being in the virtual world.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8919, 298, 'Which format represents 3D objects for AR/VR scenes?', '3D models with meshes and textures', 'Plain text files', 'CSV tables', 'Audio files only', 'A', '3D assets are defined by geometry, textures and materials.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8920, 298, 'Which of the following is a common VR application?', 'Virtual training simulations', 'Word processing', 'Email sending', 'Printing labels', 'A', 'VR is widely used for immersive training in safe, simulated environments.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8921, 298, 'Which challenge is most important for comfortable VR?', 'Maintaining high frame rates and low latency to avoid motion sickness', 'Adding more colours', 'Increasing text size', 'Using larger fonts', 'A', 'Latency and frame rate are critical because mismatch causes disorientation.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8922, 298, 'Which technique anchors virtual objects to the real world in AR?', 'Tracking real-world features and placing objects relative to them', 'Drawing objects on a fixed screen', 'Using static images', 'Disabling the camera', 'A', 'Tracking lets virtual content stay aligned as the user moves.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8923, 298, 'Which concern is most important when users spend long sessions in a shared virtual space?', 'Social safety, moderation and identity protections', 'The screen brightness', 'The avatar colours', 'The chat font', 'A', 'Persistent social spaces require safety and moderation policies.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8924, 298, 'Which trade-off dominates AR on mobile devices?', 'Real-time tracking accuracy versus battery and processing load', 'Screen size versus storage', 'Text width versus padding', 'Network speed versus cable length', 'A', 'Continuous tracking is compute-hungry, straining mobile batteries.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8925, 298, 'Which term describes a virtual world that keeps existing when a user logs off?', 'Persistence', 'Resolution', 'Serialisation', 'Compression', 'A', 'Persistent worlds continue to evolve even when individual users leave.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8926, 298, 'Which technology helps VR track the position of a user''s head?', 'Six degrees of freedom (6DoF) tracking', 'A clock', 'A keyboard', 'A mouse pad', 'A', '6DoF tracking captures position and rotation of the head in 3D space.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8927, 298, 'Which rendering technique is key to convincing AR occlusions?', 'Depth sensing so virtual objects hide behind real ones', 'Skipping shadows', 'Using flat colours', 'Disabling the camera', 'A', 'Depth information lets virtual objects correctly occlude behind real objects.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8928, 298, 'Which principle reduces VR motion sickness during movement?', 'Matching virtual motion to physical motion and minimising vestibular mismatch', 'Spinning the camera', 'Teleporting randomly', 'Increasing brightness', 'A', 'Aligning visual and physical motion reduces sensory conflict that causes nausea.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8929, 298, 'Which concern most affects the accessibility of AR/VR experiences?', 'Supporting users with disabilities through alternative controls and settings', 'The store rating', 'The game title', 'The package size', 'A', 'Inclusive design makes immersive experiences usable by everyone.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8930, 298, 'Which architecture best supports a metaverse where thousands of users interact simultaneously?', 'Distributed spatial servers with spatial partitioning and interest management', 'One server per user', 'No servers', 'A single shared screen', 'A', 'Spatial partitioning and interest management let big virtual worlds scale.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8931, 298, 'Which technique synchronises the state of a shared virtual object across users?', 'Deterministic simulation and authoritative server replica consistency', 'Sending every frame to everyone', 'Ignoring conflicts', 'Local-only updates', 'A', 'Autoritative servers and deterministic simulation keep the world consistent.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8932, 298, 'Which strategy most reduces bandwidth when streaming rich VR scenes?', 'Foveated rendering that renders only where the user looks in high detail', 'Sending full-resolution video', 'Tripling the bitrate', 'Streaming everything uncompressed', 'A', 'Foveated rendering follows eye gaze, concentrating detail where vision is sharpest.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8933, 298, 'Which latency target is generally required for convincing VR?', 'Motion-to-photon latency around 20 ms or less', 'One second', 'Ten seconds', 'One minute', 'A', 'Very low motion-to-photon latency is essential to preserve presence and comfort.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8934, 298, 'Which concern is most important when building economies inside the metaverse?', 'Trust, ownership and interoperability of virtual goods', 'The font of prices', 'The shop layout', 'The login button', 'A', 'Trustworthy ownership and interoperable assets sustain virtual economies.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8935, 298, 'Which security threat is most relevant to a persistent social virtual world?', 'Harassment, impersonation and abuse inside the shared space', 'Disk fragmentation', 'Printer jams', 'Screen glare', 'A', 'Persistent social spaces face abuse risks that require governance and moderation.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8936, 298, 'Which data protection concern is heightened by AR devices that see the real world?', 'Continuous capture of the user''s surroundings must be handled transparently', 'The device''s colour', 'The battery size', 'The charging port', 'A', 'AR cameras capture private spaces, so consent and data minimisation are vital.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8937, 298, 'Which technique most improves the realism of virtual avatars?', 'Animation blending and facial capture driven by the user''s expressions', 'Using static mannequins', 'Increasing polygon count alone', 'Disabling facial features', 'A', 'Avatar realism comes from natural motion and expression, not just geometry.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8938, 298, 'Which interoperability standard helps virtual worlds share assets?', 'Open standards such as glTF for portable 3D assets', 'Proprietary formats only', 'Plain text files', 'Screen recordings', 'A', 'Portable standards like glTF let assets move between different worlds.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8939, 298, 'Which testing approach best validates an AR application''s tracking robustness?', 'Testing across varied lighting, surfaces and device hardware', 'Testing in one fixed lab', 'Testing only in daylight', 'Testing with no movement', 'A', 'Real-world variability in lighting and surfaces must be tested to ensure robustness.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8940, 298, 'Which research direction most directly advances immersive user experience?', 'Reducing latency and improving tracking accuracy and rendering realism', 'Adding more menu tabs', 'Making the interface denser', 'Increasing loading screens', 'A', 'Latency, tracking and rendering are the core levers of immersion.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8941, 299, 'What is green computing?', 'Designing and using computers and IT in an environmentally sustainable way', 'Building green-coloured hardware', 'Using plants near servers', 'Recycling paper only', 'A', 'Green computing reduces the environmental footprint of computing throughout its lifecycle.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8942, 299, 'Which term describes the energy a device consumes while active?', 'Operational energy', 'Embodied energy', 'Dark energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'A', 'Operational energy is the power consumed during use of a device.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8943, 299, 'Which practice is an example of energy-efficient computing?', 'Enabling power-saving modes on idle machines', 'Keeping screens at maximum brightness', 'Running servers 24/7 idle', 'Using older inefficient hardware', 'A', 'Power management reduces the energy wasted by idle or lightly loaded systems.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8944, 299, 'Which metric measures carbon emissions produced per unit of computing work?', 'Carbon intensity or carbon footprint', 'Clock speed', 'Screen resolution', 'Bandwidth', 'A', 'Carbon intensity quantifies emissions per unit of energy or computing output.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8945, 299, 'What does e-waste refer to?', 'Discarded electronic devices and components', 'Broken keyboards in the bin', 'Deleted files', 'Spam emails', 'A', 'E-waste is end-of-life electronics whose disposal needs careful handling.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8946, 299, 'Which practice best reduces e-waste?', 'Repairing and extending the life of devices', 'Buying new devices yearly', 'Throwing devices in landfill', 'Hoarding broken devices', 'A', 'Repair and longevity reduce the volume of electronics discarded.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8947, 299, 'Which of the following is a way data centres reduce energy use?', 'Using efficient cooling and hardware virtualisation', 'Lowering the temperature to freezing', 'Keeping empty servers on', 'Disabling power management', 'A', 'Efficient cooling and consolidation reduce the energy per workload.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8948, 299, 'What is embodied energy in IT?', 'The energy used to manufacture and transport a device', 'The energy used while playing videos', 'The idle power draw', 'The standby drain', 'A', 'Embodied energy covers manufacturing, materials and transport of hardware.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8949, 299, 'Which renewable energy source is commonly used to power green data centres?', 'Solar or wind power', 'Coal', 'Diesel generators', 'Kerosene lamps', 'A', 'Green data centres increasingly rely on renewable sources like solar and wind.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8950, 299, 'Which simple action reduces a computer''s standby energy drain?', 'Switching devices off when not in use', 'Leaving them on forever', 'Using screen savers', 'Increasing brightness', 'A', 'Powering down idle devices eliminates standby consumption.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8951, 299, 'A data centre wants to reduce its carbon footprint. Which change has the most direct impact?', 'Switching to renewable energy and improving cooling efficiency', 'Buying more servers', 'Using faster monitors', 'Adding more screens', 'A', 'Energy source and efficiency directly drive the centre''s emissions.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8952, 299, 'Which computing practice most reduces energy in a busy cloud estate?', 'Right-sizing instances and scaling down idle capacity', 'Running instances 24/7 at full size', 'Doubling capacity', 'Disabling autoscaling', 'A', 'Right-sizing and elastic scaling cut the energy wasted by over-provisioning.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8953, 299, 'Which approach best reduces the energy cost of model training?', 'Using efficient architectures and stopping training once accuracy plateaus', 'Training models forever', 'Using larger batches blindly', 'Retraining daily', 'A', 'Efficient architectures and early stopping avoid wasted compute and energy.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8954, 299, 'Which of the following best describes PUE (Power Usage Effectiveness)?', 'The ratio of total data centre energy to IT equipment energy', 'The number of power cables', 'A measure of screen brightness', 'A network latency metric', 'A', 'PUE near 1.0 means little energy is wasted on cooling and overhead.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8955, 299, 'Which lifecycle stage contributes most to a smartphone''s carbon footprint in many analyses?', 'Manufacturing and embodied emissions', 'Standby power', 'Charging once', 'Screen cleaning', 'A', 'For smartphones, manufacturing emissions often dominate total lifecycle footprint.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8956, 299, 'Which strategy best reduces the environmental cost of data storage?', 'Tiered storage with cold data moved to energy-efficient archives', 'Storing everything hot forever', 'Duplicating all data', 'Keeping servers at full load', 'A', 'Tiered storage keeps frequently accessed data hot and moves cold data to low-power storage.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8957, 299, 'Which software practice most reduces energy consumption?', 'Writing efficient algorithms that reduce unnecessary computation', 'Adding more polling loops', 'Using busy-waits', 'Never optimising', 'A', 'Efficient algorithms do the same work with less CPU, cutting energy.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8958, 299, 'Which appraisal best estimates a system''s total sustainability impact?', 'Lifecycle assessment covering manufacture, use and disposal', 'Looking only at the purchase price', 'Measuring screen brightness', 'Counting cables', 'A', 'Lifecycle assessment captures the full environmental footprint of IT.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8959, 299, 'Which practice most directly lowers the carbon cost of the software you deploy?', 'Deploying to data centres powered by low-carbon energy', 'Deploying everywhere equally', 'Ignoring the location', 'Deploying at night only', 'A', 'Choosing low-carbon regions reduces emissions regardless of load.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8960, 299, 'Which design decision most reduces the energy of a server fleet over its lifetime?', 'Optimising utilisation and refreshing to modern energy-efficient hardware', 'Buying the cheapest old servers', 'Running at 10 percent utilisation', 'Ignoring hardware efficiency', 'A', 'High utilisation plus efficient hardware yields the best energy per workload.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8961, 299, 'Which scheduling strategy cuts energy by running jobs when carbon is cheapest?', 'Carbon-aware scheduling that shifts flexible jobs to low-carbon times', 'Running jobs instantly always', 'Ignoring energy prices', 'Running everything at peak', 'A', 'Carbon-aware scheduling delays flexible work to moments of clean, cheap power.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8962, 299, 'Which trade-off must a green computing strategy balance?', 'Cost, performance and energy versus the carbon benefit', 'Only the logo colour', 'Only battery size', 'Only screen size', 'A', 'Sustainability choices trade against cost and performance, so they must be engineered carefully.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8963, 299, 'Which research approach best quantifies the actual carbon impact of an AI model?', 'Tracking energy use during training and inference with a carbon accounting tool', 'Guessing by model size', 'Estimating from paper count', 'Measuring screen time', 'A', 'Measured energy and carbon accounting give a real picture of an AI model''s footprint.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8964, 299, 'Which practice most effectively extends the useful life of enterprise hardware?', 'Modular design and repairability with standardised spare parts', 'Soldering everything together', 'Disabling repair services', 'Sealing devices shut', 'A', 'Repairable, modular hardware can be serviced and reused instead of replaced.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8965, 299, 'Which end-of-life practice best reduces the environmental harm of electronics?', 'Responsible recycling that recovers materials and prevents toxic leakage', 'Burning devices for energy', 'Burying them in landfills', 'Sinking them in water', 'A', 'Proper recycling recovers metals and keeps hazardous materials out of the environment.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8966, 299, 'Which architecture decision most reduces wasted energy in a software system?', 'Event-driven and serverless design that scales to zero when idle', 'Always-on dummy services', 'Constant polling at high rate', 'Fixed oversized clusters', 'A', 'Serverless and event-driven designs consume energy only while actually working.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8967, 299, 'Which metric best compares the sustainability of two equivalent cloud providers?', 'Their renewable-energy percentage and reported carbon intensity of supplied power', 'Their website colour', 'Their billing page', 'Their domain length', 'A', 'Renewable usage and carbon intensity reveal the real environmental performance of each provider.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8968, 299, 'Which strategy best reduces the carbon cost of streaming video at scale?', 'Efficient encoding and edge caching that cut repeated transfer and transcoding', 'Streaming at maximum bitrate', 'Sending every frame from the origin', 'Disabling compression', 'A', 'Compression and caching slash the network and compute energy of video delivery.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8969, 299, 'Which governance practice makes an organisation''s green computing claims trustworthy?', 'Independent verification and transparent sustainability reporting', 'Unverified marketing slogans', 'Hidden metrics', 'Oral promises', 'A', 'Verified, transparent reporting guards against greenwashing.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8970, 299, 'Which design principle best guides an environmentally conscious system architect?', 'Minimising waste at every layer, from code to cooling', 'Maximising redundancy everywhere', 'Ignoring energy', 'Prioritising raw speed always', 'A', 'Sustainability-minded architecture reduces resource waste across the whole stack.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8971, 300, 'What is a research problem in a computing thesis?', 'A clear question or gap that the research aims to address', 'A programming error', 'A network issue', 'A typo in the report', 'A', 'A research problem defines what question the study will answer.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8972, 300, 'What is a literature review?', 'A critical summary of prior work related to the research topic', 'A list of books in the library', 'A syntax check', 'A backup of papers', 'A', 'A literature review synthesises existing research to position new work.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8973, 300, 'Which section of a research paper states the aim and scope of the study?', 'Introduction', 'Appendix', 'References', 'Index', 'A', 'The introduction frames the problem, aims and scope of the study.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8974, 300, 'What is a hypothesis in research?', 'A testable statement predicting an outcome', 'A final conclusion', 'A citation format', 'A data table', 'A', 'A hypothesis is a falsifiable prediction that experiments can test.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8975, 300, 'Which type of study systematically evaluates a research question using evidence?', 'Empirical research', 'Speculative writing', 'Marketing copy', 'Personal opinion', 'A', 'Empirical research gathers and analyses data to answer questions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8976, 300, 'What is an experiment in computing research?', 'A controlled procedure that tests a hypothesis', 'A compiler run', 'A formatting pass', 'A login attempt', 'A', 'Experiments isolate variables to test predictions under controlled conditions.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8977, 300, 'Which term describes repeating a study to confirm its findings?', 'Reproduction or replication', 'Compilation', 'Deployment', 'Serialisation', 'A', 'Replication repeats research to verify that results hold.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8978, 300, 'Which of the following is a reputable source for computing research?', 'Peer-reviewed journals and conferences', 'Random blog posts', 'Anonymous forums', 'Social media memes', 'A', 'Peer-reviewed venues verify quality before publication.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8979, 300, 'What role does a citation play in a research paper?', 'A reference to a source that supports a claim', 'A syntax error', 'A database index', 'A screen capture', 'A', 'Citations credit sources and let readers trace the evidence.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8980, 300, 'Which component of a paper summarises the study in a short self-contained paragraph?', 'The abstract', 'The appendix', 'The acknowledgements', 'The cover', 'A', 'The abstract condenses the paper''s purpose, method and results.', 'BEGINNER', 1, 'MCQ', 1),
(8981, 300, 'A researcher finds their measured result does not match their hypothesis. What is the correct approach?', 'Report the result honestly and discuss why it differs', 'Hide the result', 'Fake matching data', 'Delete the experiment', 'A', 'Honest reporting of unexpected results is essential to scientific integrity.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8982, 300, 'Which metric most appropriately measures the benefit of a new algorithm?', 'Comparative evaluation against established baselines on standard benchmarks', 'The number of lines of code', 'The developer''s opinion', 'The repository stars', 'A', 'Benchmarks and baselines give objective, comparable evidence of improvement.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8983, 300, 'Which practice best ensures another researcher can reproduce your experiments?', 'Publishing code, data and a clear experimental protocol', 'Keeping everything private', 'Describing results vaguely', 'Deleting old data', 'A', 'Open code, data and protocols are the basis of reproducible research.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8984, 300, 'Which statistical concern must a researcher address when comparing two models?', 'Whether the performance difference is statistically significant', 'The models'' names', 'The colour of the charts', 'The team size', 'A', 'Significance testing determines whether differences are real or due to chance.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8985, 300, 'What is the main purpose of a research ethics review?', 'To protect participants and ensure the study is conducted responsibly', 'To shorten the paper', 'To increase citations', 'To choose a font', 'A', 'Ethics review safeguards participants and research integrity.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8986, 300, 'Which choice best avoids bias in an experimental comparison?', 'Using identical datasets and controlled conditions for all methods', 'Tuning only your own method', 'Choosing favourable cases', 'Selecting the best result', 'A', 'Controlled, fair comparisons prevent experimenter bias.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8987, 300, 'Which section presents and interprets the numerical outcomes of an experiment?', 'Results and discussion', 'The bibliography', 'The index', 'The cover letter', 'A', 'Results report findings while discussion interprets their meaning.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8988, 300, 'Which tooling practice most helps others reuse your research code?', 'Documented, packaged code with clear setup instructions and a licence', 'Obfuscated scripts', 'Unlabelled notebooks', 'Password-protected repos', 'A', 'Usable, licensed code makes research findings actionable.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8989, 300, 'Which criterion helps a researcher judge whether a conference paper is credible?', 'Peer review process and acceptance standards', 'The number of pages', 'The abstract length', 'The paper colour', 'A', 'Rigorous peer review indicates a credible venue.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8990, 300, 'Which research design best isolates the contribution of a new component in a large system?', 'Ablation studies that remove the component and measure the effect', 'Evaluating the whole system only', 'Never comparing', 'Using only anecdotes', 'A', 'Ablation studies pinpoint exactly what a component contributes.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8991, 300, 'Which practice most strengthens the validity of a user study in computing?', 'A representative sample with controlled tasks and statistical analysis', 'A handful of friends', 'Only developers as participants', 'Unsupervised self-reports', 'A', 'Representative samples and proper design make user-study conclusions trustworthy.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8992, 300, 'Which threat to validity is most common when testing ML models on one dataset?', 'Overfitting that fails to generalise to other datasets', 'Too much data', 'Too many papers', 'Excessive compute', 'A', 'Single-dataset evaluation risks results that do not generalise.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8993, 300, 'Which approach best positions a research paper within the existing literature?', 'Clearly stating how the work differs from and extends prior results', 'Ignoring prior work', 'Copying the abstract of another paper', 'Hiding related work', 'A', 'Explicit positioning against prior work demonstrates novelty and relevance.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8994, 300, 'Which consideration is most important when choosing a research topic?', 'That it addresses a real gap and is feasible to investigate', 'That it sounds impressive', 'That it is popular on social media', 'That it is easy', 'A', 'A good topic balances importance with feasibility.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8995, 300, 'Which methodology best evaluates a distributed-systems claim at scale?', 'Controlled experiments on testbeds or cloud resources with realistic workloads', 'A single laptop test', 'Theoretical reasoning only', 'A small unit test', 'A', 'Realistic-scale experiments provide credible evidence for systems claims.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8996, 300, 'Which ethical concern is most relevant when mining public web data for research?', 'User privacy, consent expectations and terms-of-service compliance', 'The download speed', 'The file format', 'The server region', 'A', 'Web scraping must respect privacy, consent and platform rules.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8997, 300, 'Which approach most improves the reliability of a research conclusion?', 'Repeating experiments across varied conditions and analysing error margins', 'Trusting a single run', 'Selecting the best run', 'Removing outliers silently', 'A', 'Multiple runs and error analysis give robust, defensible conclusions.', 'INTERMEDIATE', 2, 'MCQ', 1),
(8998, 300, 'Which publication practice most benefits the research community?', 'Releasing negative results that prevent others from repeating dead ends', 'Hiding negative results', 'Publishing only wins', 'Duplicating known results', 'A', 'Negative results save others'' time and improve the field''s efficiency.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(8999, 300, 'Which criterion best distinguishes a genuine research contribution from an engineering task?', 'A generalisable insight or method that advances understanding beyond one instance', 'The number of features built', 'The code''s complexity', 'The number of screens', 'A', 'Research advances generalisable knowledge rather than solving one instance.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1),
(9000, 300, 'Which emerging trend most plausibly shapes computing research in the coming decade?', 'Convergence of AI with quantum, edge and sustainable computing', 'The decline of all computing', 'Removing AI from research', 'Returning to punch cards', 'A', 'Interdisciplinary convergence across AI, quantum, edge and sustainability defines the research frontier.', 'ADVANCED', 3, 'MCQ', 1);
